 cargo test -p <crate> --lib <module>::<test>` — scoped unit/integration tests; failures map cleanly to a single task.\n- `cd daemon && cargo bench --bench <name> -- --warm-up-time 1 --measurement-time 3` — quick benches that catch P99 regressions on hot paths.\n- `cd extension && npm run compile && npm run lint && npm test` — extension TypeScript compile, lint, and runner.\n- `test -f <path>` + `grep -q '## Heading' <path>` — doc-producing tasks confirm structural completeness.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib contract_tests::<x>` — contract-gate tasks have their own dedicated test module.\n\nThe final readiness review (R89) runs the full `cargo test --workspace`, full extension compile/lint/test, and asserts every prior review file exists and is closed.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Out-of-scope (per spec audit)\n\nThe spec lists nothing out of scope. This plan plans all of it. The \"First Implementation Slice\" steps in the spec (event log → memory graph → retrieval V1 → consolidation → verification) appear first inside their phases, but every later phase still ships in this build per the execution philosophy.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Files this plan produces (summary)\n\nThe complete file inventory for the build is the union of every `files_expected` array in [`tasks.json`](tasks.json). Major groupings:\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/identity/**` — Phase 1.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/**` — Phase 2.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/**` — Phase 3.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/**` — Phase 4.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/**` — Phase 5.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/consolidation/**` — Phase 6.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/verification/**` — Phase 7.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/**` — Phase 9.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/hardening/**` — Phase 11.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/contract_tests/**` — cross-layer gates.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/**` and `rpc/memory_v2/**` — Phase 8.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/event_capture.rs`, `working_memory_tool.rs`, `metrics_surface.rs`, `mcp_schema_tests.rs`, `mcp_compat_tests.rs` — instrumentation + contract tests.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/bin/lattice_report.rs` — CLI regression report.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/benches/**` — baseline + benchmark suites.\n- `extension/src/review/**` — Phase 10 review UI.\n- `extension/src/test/**` — extension UI smoke + contract tests.\n- `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-*.md` — design notes per phase.\n- `docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-*.md` — operator + migration + benchmark + UI guides + runbook + recovery playbook.\n- `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R*.md` — all review outputs.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Where the per-task briefs live\n\nEvery task (`Txx`) and every review (`Rxx`) has a markdown file under [`tasks/`](tasks/) with: goal, prerequisites, inputs, steps, files-expected, verification commands, coding-standard checks, and definition-of-done for that task. Executors should open the next pending task's file before touching code.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T71.log:1973:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2731:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:743:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_0139kC52VEu3YD1r5ACQ9dAL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016gvQUZeZmcnYPT16NbkckX","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T61.md","content":"# T61 — Phase 8 — Tool surface audit + final MCP reference doc (collapse overlapping tools per spec §MCP discipline)\n\n**Phase:** 8\n**Type:** docs\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** T56, T57, T58, T59, T60\n**Spec anchor:** [§MCP Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#9-mcp-surface)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task produces the audited final MCP tool surface for the cognitive workspace fork: an audit doc recording which tools were collapsed and why, and the canonical reference doc enumerating every tool the daemon ships, its render modes, expansion handles, budget controls, deprecation status, and shim policy. Spec §MCP Surface \"Tool surface discipline\" makes this audit binding: \"ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients … The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\" Without this task, the Phase 8 work ships an unaudited surface and Phase 9 metrics regress against the wrong set of tool names.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Tool surface discipline: ten new memory tools is a meaningful cognitive load for clients. Before shipping Phase 8, audit whether `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` can collapse into a single tool with an `action` parameter, and whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n> \"Every tool response should support: compact rendering, full structured JSON, context handles, stable expansion targets, budget controls, diagnostic explanations where useful.\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n> \"Required workflow tools: `get_context_capsule`, `prepare_change`, `plan_edit`, `trace_scenario`, `diagnose_failure`, `find_relevant_tests`, `impact_from_diff`, `get_docs_capsule`, `get_backlinks`, `expand_context`, `get_working_set_context`, `get_repo_playbook`, `search_symbols`, `search_memory`, `record_workflow_outcome`.\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T56 (`workflow_v2/` edit-planning tools) — final names and request/response schemas.\n  - T57 (`workflow_v2/` discovery tools) — final names and request/response schemas.\n  - T58 (`memory_v2/` get_task_memory + save_memory + unified `propose_memory_evolution`).\n  - T59 (`memory_v2/` unified `verify_explain_memory` + `list_memory_conflicts`).\n  - T60 (`memory_v2/` consolidate_session + get_memory_metrics + get_event_trace).\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` (from T02) — must be referenced for deprecation policy and shim removal cadence.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — current registered tool list (43 today); the audit doc must reconcile every entry against the final list.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Single source of truth, §No broken windows; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references — the reference doc is the authoritative MCP tool listing and must be cited by every subsequent doc.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §MCP Surface in full and quote the \"Tool surface discipline\" paragraph verbatim in `2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md` as the framing for the audit.\n2. In `2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md`, write a `## Audit findings` section that lists every collapse decision made during Phase 8: (a) `propose_memory_evolution` + `apply_memory_evolution` → unified `propose_memory_evolution` with `action` parameter (per T58); (b) `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` → unified `verify_explain_memory` with `mode` parameter (per T59); plus any further collapses identified while reviewing T56–T60. For each collapse, record: rationale, the spec sentence authorizing it, the deprecation shim policy, and the shim-removal deadline.\n3. In `2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md`, write a `## Collapsed tools` section that is a table or bullet list mapping each spec-nominal tool name to its actual implementation: `apply_memory_evolution → propose_memory_evolution(action=\"apply\")`; `verify_memory → verify_explain_memory(mode=\"verify\")`; `explain_memory → verify_explain_memory(mode=\"explain\")`; and any others identified. Each row links to the implementing file under `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/` or `workflow_v2/`.\n4. In `2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md`, write a `## Decisions not to collapse` section documenting any spec-nominal pair that was considered but NOT collapsed (e.g. `get_context_capsule` vs `get_docs_capsule` remain separate because they anchor on distinct identity types), with rationale.\n5. In `2026-05-16-mcp-tool-reference.md`, write a `## Final tool list` section enumerating every tool the daemon ships after Phase 8. For each tool, include: canonical name, one-paragraph description, request schema summary, response schema summary, supported render modes (`compact` / `full` / `diagnostic` / handles), budget controls, emitted Phase 2 events, deprecation status, implementing file path. The list must include every spec §MCP Surface \"Required workflow tools\" and every new memory tool (counting the collapsed names) plus the deprecation shims kept active per the T02 compatibility policy.\n6. In `2026-05-16-mcp-tool-reference.md`, add sections `## Render modes`, `## Expansion handles`, `## Budget controls`, `## Deprecation policy`, each citing the corresponding spec heading.\n7. Cross-link `2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md` ↔ `2026-05-16-mcp-tool-reference.md` and reference back to spec §MCP Surface, §MCP Tool Contract Principles, and the T02 compatibility policy by exact heading.\n8. Confirm no spec §MCP Surface \"Required workflow tools\" entry is missing from the reference. Confirm no implemented tool is missing from the reference. The reference doc is single source of truth.\n9. Confirm both docs respect the Cadres coding standard `## Hard limits` (file ≤800 lines); split into two docs is already mandated by the verification commands.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-tool-reference.md` — canonical reference enumerating every shipped MCP tool, render modes, expansion handles, budget controls, deprecation status, and implementing file paths.\n- `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md` — audit findings recording every tool collapse decision per spec §MCP Surface \"Tool surface discipline,\" including the table of collapsed tools and the deprecation shim policy.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-tool-reference.md` — confirms the reference doc exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md` — confirms the audit doc exists at the harness path.\n- `grep -q '## Final tool list' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-tool-reference.md` — confirms the reference enumerates the final tool list under a dedicated heading rather than burying it.\n- `grep -q '## Audit findings' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md` — confirms the audit findings section exists under a dedicated heading.\n- `grep -q '## Collapsed tools' docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md` — confirms the collapsed-tools mapping is recorded under a dedicated heading rather than scattered through prose.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (verify with `wc -l docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-tool-reference.md docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-surface-audit.md`).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10 — not applicable (docs task).\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code.\n- No unjustified `// allow(...)` or suppressions.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming — applies to any tool/schema names mentioned in the docs; they must match the implementations in T56–T60.\n- For schema changes: the reference doc is the single source of truth; every divergence between docs and code is a contract gate failure caught by R64.\n- Doc citation: cite the exact `## MCP Surface`, `## MCP Tool Contract Principles`, and `## Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2` headings from the spec; reference `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` from T02 for deprecation policy.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every collapse decision is recorded; every shipped tool is listed; no \"to be added later\" placeholders for tool entries.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed and the T02 compatibility policy was cross-referenced.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4085,"cache_read_input_tokens":77188,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4085,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"1b19303a-2693-4a8c-9024-c6b34eb8d8d5","request_id":"req_011Cb7N83CrnmzCR4uLXc6tv","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T71.log:1974:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2736:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:782:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_019ScAFhwj15KgrT4jB8Uvi8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TwdU3puoByR54shmvCc2Fc","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/R78.md","content":"# R78 — Frontend review — Phase 10 human review UI (UI-spec compliance)\n\n**Phase:** Review for Phase 10\n**Type:** review\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** T71, T72, T73, T74, T75, T76, T77\n**Spec anchor:** [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-10-human-review-and-extension-ux)\n**Standards reviewed:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); UI specification (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md`); definition-of-done checklist (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md`).\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis review certifies that the Phase 10 human review UI ships compliant with the shared UI specification, the Lattice coding standard, the spec's mandatory operator view set, and the i18n discipline that all user-facing strings live in a single catalog. It is the gate that R79 (contract gate) and R88 (definition-of-done gate) depend on: a frontend that bypasses `<dialog>`, swallows errors, or inlines English literals would force later phases into rework. Passing this review means the review surface is enterprise-grade on day one per the execution philosophy in `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Inputs to read\n\n- Every artifact produced by T71–T77:\n  - `extension/src/review/reviewPanel.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/i18n/en.json`\n  - `extension/src/review/i18n/index.ts` (i18n helper)\n  - `extension/src/review/memoryInbox.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/components/MemoryRow.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/components/StatusBadge.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/promotionQueue.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/contradictionQueue.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/components/ProposalDialog.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/staleView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/evidenceInspector.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/eventTraceView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/retrievalExplanationView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/consolidationQueueView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/indexingHealthView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/workspaceGraphHealthView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/test/review.test.ts`\n  - `extension/src/test/runTest.ts`\n  - `extension/package.json` (review view + command contribution diffs)\n- Spec sections this phase claims to satisfy: `## 10. Human Review Surface`, `## Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX`.\n- Shared standards: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (hard limits, error handling, single source of truth), `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md` (all sections — §3 accessibility, §6 lists & tables, §7 modals, §8 state management, §11 visual standards, §14 i18n, §16 telemetry), `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md`.\n\n## Review procedure\n\n1. Open every file produced by T71–T77 and confirm the eleven operator views from spec §10 are all reachable from `ReviewPanelProvider`: memory inbox, proposed promotions, proposed contradictions, stale memory list, memory evidence view, event trace view, retrieval explanation view, usefulness metrics, workspace graph health, indexing health, consolidation queue. (Usefulness metrics may live in the existing sidebar from `sidebar.ts` if R70 confirmed that surface; either way the link must be reachable.)\n2. Grep every `.ts` file under `extension/src/review/` for `window.confirm(`, `window.prompt(`, `window.alert(` — every hit is a blocker per `ui-specification.md` §7. Grep for `<dialog` to confirm the modals from T73 and T75 use native `<dialog>` elements.\n3. Grep every `.ts` file under `extension/src/review/` for inline English literals in JSX-equivalent strings: look for quoted strings inside webview HTML template literals that are not wrapped in `i18n.t(...)`. Any hit is a blocker per `ui-specification.md` §14.\n4. Open `extension/src/review/i18n/en.json` and confirm namespaces exist for: panel, statusBadge, memoryInbox, promotionQueue, contradictionQueue, proposalDialog, staleView, evidenceInspector, eventTraceView, retrievalExplanationView, consolidationQueueView, indexingHealthView, workspaceGraphHealthView. Spot-check that every column header, action label, empty state, and error state from the eleven views has a corresponding key.\n5. Open every mutation handler (accept/reject in `ProposalDialog`, re-verify in `staleView`/`evidenceInspector`, retry in `consolidationQueueView`, recompute in `workspaceGraphHealthView`) and confirm each is wrapped in `try/catch` with `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` plus an inline error state. Silent `.catch(() => {})` or bare `await` without surrounding handler is a blocker per `ui-specification.md` §8.5 and `coding.md` §Error handling.\n6. Confirm `StatusBadge.ts` covers all eight verification statuses from spec §8 (`verified`, `unverified`, `in_review`, `stale`, `contradicted`, `superseded`, `expired`, `invalidated`) and every additional badge family (proposal status, job status, decision badge in retrieval explanation). Confirm the file is the only place `verification-status → color/label` mapping lives.\n7. Open every table (memory inbox, promotion queue, contradiction queue, stale view, event trace, candidate table in retrieval explanation, consolidation queue, per-family counts in graph health) and confirm sortable columns use the `SortableHeader` + `useTableSort` equivalents exposed by T71 (per `ui-specification.md` §6.2). Confirm pagination is present on every list per `ui-specification.md` §6.1.\n8. Open every interactive surface and confirm it renders loading, empty, and error states explicitly (per `ui-specification.md` §8.2, §8.3, §8.5).\n9. Grep for `prompt(`, raw `<select>`, and `type=\"number\"` ID inputs — any hit for ID-bearing fields is a blocker. Confirm filter pickers use `vscode.window.showQuickPick`.\n10. Run `wc -l` on every file produced by T71–T77 and confirm no file exceeds 800 lines per `coding.md`.\n11. Grep all files for `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error` without justification — each hit is a blocker per `coding.md` §No broken windows.\n12. Run `cd extension && npm install && npm run compile && npm run lint && npm test` and confirm everything passes.\n13. Cross-check that the smoke tests from T77 cover every subview from T72–T76 (activation, view registration, mount, empty data, bridge error).\n\n## Required sections in the review output file\n\nThe reviewer writes to `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` with these mandatory headings (each must contain real findings, not \"n/a\"):\n\n- `## Spec alignment` — phase-by-phase coverage matrix for spec §10: one row per required operator view (eleven views), evidence column citing the file/function that delivers the view, status PASS / PASS-WITH-CAVEATS / BLOCK.\n- `## Coding-standard alignment` — line-by-line audit vs. `coding.md` hard limits: file lengths (`wc -l` table for every Phase 10 file), function-length grep, forbidden-token grep (`TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, unjustified suppressions), error-handling audit (every mutation `try/catch` confirmed), single-source-of-truth audit (status-badge mapping in one place, i18n catalog as single source).\n- `## UI-specification compliance` — walk every section of `ui-specification.md` that applies to the review UI in order. Each subsection states the rule, lists the artifacts checked, and the verdict:\n  - §3 Accessibility baseline — focus management, keyboard, ARIA, target size, focus rings, live regions on status badges.\n  - §6 Lists & tables — pagination present on every list; sortable headers via `SortableHeader` + `useTableSort` equivalents; column standards.\n  - §7 Modals & dialogs — every modal native `<dialog>`; no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`; typed-confirm on high-scope destructive actions in `ProposalDialog`.\n  - §8 State management — loading, empty, error states on every surface; every mutation has `try/catch` + `vscode.window.showErrorMessage`; error message style obeys Nielsen #9.\n  - §11 Visual standards — status badges use the single shared component; semantic color tokens, not hard-coded colors.\n  - §12 Data display — dates via `Intl.DateTimeFormat`; truncation on long content.\n  - §14 Internationalization — all user-facing strings via `i18n.t(...)`; no inline literals; ICU placeholders on interpolated strings; no string concatenation for user text.\n  - §16 Telemetry-friendly markup — `data-testid` on every interactive element; format `data-testid=\"review-<view>-<action>\"`.\n- `## i18n coverage` — table of every user-facing string the review UI emits, with the i18n key and the namespace; flag any inline literal that escaped catalog wrapping as a blocker.\n- `## Findings` — issues found, severity (`blocker`, `major`, `minor`), suggested follow-up tasks. Follow-up tasks must be opened in this build (do not defer outside).\n- `## Verdict` — `PASS`, `PASS-WITH-FOLLOWUP-TASK-Txx`, or `BLOCK` (with named blockers).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms the review markdown exists where downstream tasks can read it.\n- `grep -q '## Spec alignment' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms spec alignment is a dedicated section, not prose.\n- `grep -q '## UI-specification compliance' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms UI-spec compliance walked explicitly.\n- `grep -q '## i18n coverage' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms i18n coverage table present.\n- `grep -q '## Verdict' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms the review concludes with a binding verdict.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Review markdown file exists at the path verification commands check.\n- [ ] Every required section above is present with concrete content (not \"n/a\").\n- [ ] `## Coding-standard alignment` and `## Findings` headings are also present (not gated by tasks.json verification but required for completeness).\n- [ ] If verdict is BLOCK, the blocking issues are listed and a follow-up task is opened in this build (never deferred to a future session per `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` execution philosophy).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":101320,"cache_read_input_tokens":10845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":101320,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"92e01477-56ca-4f53-90e6-aa05679be5ea","request_id":"req_011Cb7NGicAyFMeCwEAwKwU2","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T71.log:1975:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2739:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:797:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_019ScAFhwj15KgrT4jB8Uvi8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DeEj2PHYWT2tYJ3BkufH5S","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/R79.md","content":"# R79 — Contract gate — Extension ↔ Daemon review-UI MCP plumbing\n\n**Phase:** Contract gate after Phase 10\n**Type:** review\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R64, R78\n**Spec anchor:** [§9. MCP Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#9-mcp-surface), [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§MCP Tool Contract Principles](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#mcp-tool-contract-principles)\n**Standards reviewed:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis review is the contract gate between the Phase 10 review UI and the daemon MCP surface that powers it. Every method on `ReviewRpcBridge` calls a tool registered on the daemon side; this gate verifies that every method has a matching tool, that the JSON shapes round-trip end to end, that the schema is documented and regression-tested, and that no surface added by Phases 6–9 was forgotten on the extension side. The gate exists because UI compile + lint cannot detect contract drift — only an integration test that sends a real request to a real daemon and asserts the response shape can. Spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties demands \"every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested\"; this review enforces that for the review UI surface.\n\n## Inputs to read\n\n- Every artifact produced by T71–T77 (extension review surface).\n- Every artifact produced by Phase 6–9 MCP work the review UI depends on, including the R64 contract gate output for the MCP tool schema.\n- Existing files this review cross-checks:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — registered MCP tools.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs` — JSON-RPC server.\n  - `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts` — typed extension-side bridge.\n  - `extension/src/daemon.ts` — underlying JSON-RPC client.\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` (from T02) — backward-compatibility policy this gate enforces.\n- Spec sections: `## 9. MCP Surface`, `## 10. Human Review Surface`, `## MCP Tool Contract Principles`.\n- Shared standards: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (schema parity, single source of truth); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md` (backward-compatibility discipline).\n\n## Review procedure\n\n1. Enumerate every method exposed by `ReviewRpcBridge` and confirm each maps to a registered MCP tool in `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs`. Any orphan method on either side is a blocker.\n2. For every mapped pair, inspect the typed request and response shapes on the TypeScript side (`rpcBridge.ts`) and the corresponding Rust schema in `mcp.rs` (and its serde structs). Field-by-field comparison: type, required/optional, name. Drift is a blocker per `coding.md` §Schema parity.\n3. Cross-check `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` (T02) — every review-UI tool is either `stable`, `additive`, or `redesigned-with-shim`; nothing is undocumented.\n4. Create `extension/src/test/contract.test.ts` (this is the deliverable contract test). The test launches a real daemon process via the existing pattern in `extension/src/test/`, sends one round-trip request per review-UI bridge method, and asserts the response parses into the typed shape. Include happy-path requests and an error-path request per tool. Tests use the real daemon binary (`daemon/target/release/lattice`), not a mock.\n5. Run the contract suite with `cd extension && npm install && npm run compile && npm test`. Confirm every round-trip passes. If the daemon binary is not present in `extension/bin/`, the test setup builds and copies per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy: `cd daemon && cargo build --release && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/`.\n6. Confirm the contract test exercises every review-UI tool from `rpcBridge.ts`: memory listing, evidence inspection, promotion queue read + apply + reject, contradiction queue read + apply + reject, stale listing, verify_memory, event trace read, retrieval explanation read, consolidation queue read + retry, indexing health read, graph health read + recompute.\n7. Grep `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` for the corresponding tool registrations and confirm each accepts the documented request shape and returns the documented response shape.\n8. Cross-check that backward-compat shims from R64 (if any) are still in place: every legacy tool name continues to route to the new implementation.\n\n## Required sections in the review output file\n\nThe reviewer writes to `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R79-contract-extension-daemon.md` with these mandatory headings (each must contain real findings, not \"n/a\"):\n\n- `## Contract surfaces` — table of every `ReviewRpcBridge` method, the corresponding daemon tool name, the request shape, the response shape, and the spec citation. One row per method.\n- `## Round-trip evidence` — for every method, the contract-test name in `extension/src/test/contract.test.ts` that exercises it; the test output snippet showing the request, the response, and the assertion that the typed shape parsed; the daemon log line showing the request was received.\n- `## Schema surfaces` — TypeScript ↔ Rust field comparison table per tool. Columns: TS field, TS type, TS required, Rust field, Rust type, Rust required, parity verdict. Any drift is flagged.\n- `## Findings` — issues found, severity (`blocker`, `major`, `minor`), suggested follow-up tasks. Follow-up tasks must be opened in this build (do not defer outside).\n- `## Verdict` — `PASS`, `PASS-WITH-FOLLOWUP-TASK-Txx`, or `BLOCK` (with named blockers).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R79-contract-extension-daemon.md` — confirms the review markdown exists where downstream tasks can read it.\n- `test -f extension/src/test/contract.test.ts` — confirms the new contract test file exists.\n- `grep -q '## Contract surfaces' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R79-contract-extension-daemon.md` — confirms contract surfaces enumerated under a dedicated heading.\n- `grep -q '## Round-trip evidence' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R79-contract-extension-daemon.md` — confirms round-trip evidence is present.\n- `cd extension && npm run compile` — TypeScript compile (including the new contract test) succeeds.\n- `cd extension && npm test` — the contract suite plus all prior tests pass against a real daemon.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Review markdown file exists at the path verification commands check.\n- [ ] `extension/src/test/contract.test.ts` exists, compiles, and passes against the real daemon binary.\n- [ ] Every required section above is present with concrete content (not \"n/a\").\n- [ ] `## Schema surfaces` and `## Findings` are also present (not gated by tasks.json verification but required for completeness).\n- [ ] If verdict is BLOCK, the blocking issues are listed and a follow-up task is opened in this build (never deferred to a future session per `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` execution philosophy).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":101320,"cache_read_input_tokens":10845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":101320,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"ce6e8520-9b5f-42b8-a09b-8be0a2ce22df","request_id":"req_011Cb7NGicAyFMeCwEAwKwU2","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T71.log:1976:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2749:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:867:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01MPB6E3V4a7oByv1GVt2tnV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013HQsYfpzN9F19m4z35f4hY","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T69.md","content":"# T69 — Phase 9 — Metrics regression coverage tests (success-criteria targets from spec)\n\n**Phase:** 9\n**Type:** tests\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** T65, T66, T67, T68\n**Spec anchor:** [§Measurable Success Criteria](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#measurable-success-criteria)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task ships the regression tests that prove the cognitive workspace fork beats the baselines on every initial target from spec §Measurable Success Criteria. The tests run the redesigned tools (T56/T57) against the T66 fixture repos, feed the captured events into the T65 metrics collector, render the T68 regression report, and assert each spec §Measurable Success Criteria target. Without this task, the metrics infrastructure exists but no automated gate proves the system meets its stated goals — the Phase 9 DoD (\"changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\") would not be satisfied.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Initial targets: 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks; 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads; 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks; zero trusted display of known contradicted memory; zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label; 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks.\" (spec §Measurable Success Criteria)\n\n> \"Definition of done: changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote.\" (spec §Phase 9)\n\n> \"The fork should beat current Lattice on: lower tool calls per coding task; lower irrelevant context inclusion; higher relevant file/symbol recall; higher memory reuse precision; lower stale memory surfacing; better repeated-failure avoidance; better test recommendation accuracy; better user correction retention; faster diagnosis from failures; more explainable context selection.\" (spec §Measurable Success Criteria)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T65 — `MetricsCollector`, `MetricSignal`, `MetricValue`.\n  - T66 — fixture repos + `golden_anchors.json` + `cognitive_workspace_benchmark`.\n  - T67 — `metrics_surface` (per-call relevance is needed for `IrrelevantFilesOpenedPerTask` and inclusion-precision assertions).\n  - T68 — `RegressionReport`, `SuccessCriteriaThresholds`, `lattice_report` binary.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/baselines/baseline_metrics.json` (from T04) — baseline column for delta-based assertions (30% fewer, 40% fewer).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/` — module from T65.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/` and `memory_v2/` — tools under test.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard `## Hard limits`, `## Tests as documentation`, `## No broken windows`, `## Schema / model parity`.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Measurable Success Criteria and §Phase 9 DoD in full. Quote the \"Initial targets\" paragraph verbatim into a module-level doc comment on `metrics/regression_tests.rs` so the thresholds are visible at the test source.\n2. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/regression_tests.rs` containing one `#[test]` per spec §Measurable Success Criteria initial target. Each test loads the T66 fixtures, runs the redesigned tools end-to-end against the golden tasks, feeds events into the T65 `MetricsCollector`, and asserts the corresponding threshold using the T68 `SuccessCriteriaThresholds`:\n   - `test_discovery_tool_calls_at_least_30pct_below_baseline` — `ToolCallsPerSuccessfulTask` is ≥30% below the baseline value from `baseline_metrics.json` for the discovery-tool subset.\n   - `test_irrelevant_file_reads_at_least_40pct_below_baseline` — `IrrelevantFilesOpenedPerTask` is ≥40% below baseline.\n   - `test_memory_inclusion_precision_at_least_80pct` — `MemoryInclusionPrecision` ≥ 0.80 on the curated memory benchmark tasks from T66.\n   - `test_zero_trusted_display_of_contradicted_memory` — `ContradictionMissedRate` equals 0 across all fixture tasks.\n   - `test_zero_trusted_display_of_stale_memory_without_label` — `StaleMemorySurfacedRate` equals 0 across all fixture tasks (stale memories may appear, but never without an explicit stale label per spec §Phase 7 DoD).\n   - `test_relevant_test_recommendation_at_least_90pct` — `TestsRecommendedVsNeeded` recall ≥ 0.90 on the curated tasks from T66.\n3. Each test must surface a useful failure diagnostic: when an assertion fails, print the per-signal `MetricValue` plus the contributing event/memory evidence pointers from T68 so the regression is investigable without re-running.\n4. Mark long-running tests `#[ignore]` only if they exceed CI budget; the verification command runs with `-- --include-ignored` so CI executes them. Spec §Tests as documentation forbids silently skipping required coverage.\n5. Implement a helper `MetricsTestHarness` in the same file (or a sibling `regression_harness.rs`) that loads fixtures deterministically, executes a fixed tool sequence per task, captures events into an isolated `.lattice/events.db`, and returns the resulting `MetricValue`s. The harness encapsulates the deterministic-seed and fixture-freeze policy from T66 so per-test code is short.\n6. Add an explicit assertion at the top of the file that `baseline_metrics.json` exists; if not, the tests fail fast with a clear error pointing to T04 rather than passing accidentally.\n7. Workspace boundary + scope: every harness invocation runs in an isolated workspace under a temp directory; never reuses or contaminates the developer's local `.lattice/`.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/regression_tests.rs` — one `#[test]` per spec §Measurable Success Criteria initial target asserting the redesigned system beats baselines (30% discovery, 40% irrelevant reads, 80% memory precision, 0 stale/contradicted trusted display, 90% test recommendation); shared `MetricsTestHarness` for deterministic fixture execution; per-failure diagnostics via T68 evidence pointers.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/metrics/regression_tests.rs` — confirms the regression-tests file exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib metrics::regression_tests -- --include-ignored` — every spec §Measurable Success Criteria regression test passes against the T66 fixture repos.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines (split into `regression_tests.rs` + `regression_harness.rs` if needed).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: each test reads thresholds from the T68 `SuccessCriteriaThresholds` rather than hard-coding numbers in two places; signal names use the T65 `MetricSignal` enum.\n- No silent error handling — every fixture load / harness failure produces a typed error; no test uses `unwrap()` on a `Result` that could legitimately fail without diagnostic.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no `#[ignore]` without a written reason; no skipped assertions.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`MetricsTestHarness::run_fixture_task`, not `MetricsTestHarness::go`).\n- For SQLite: temp DBs only; harness cleans up on completion.\n- Observability: each test emits `tracing::info!` with `signal`, `target`, `actual`, `pass` fields on completion.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## Measurable Success Criteria` and `## Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation` in module doc comments.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every one of the six spec §Measurable Success Criteria initial targets has a corresponding `#[test]`; no target is punted with a placeholder.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3556,"cache_read_input_tokens":106980,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3556,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":51,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"3f844d71-255e-43d5-a676-4efcaa90f907","request_id":"req_011Cb7NbRkgeaFKnUV3Q7p7E","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T71.log:1977:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2754:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:913:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 19 task brief files written successfully.\n\n**File count:** 19 (T71, T72, T73, T74, T75, T76, T77, R78, R79, T80, T81, T82, T83, T84, T85, T86, R87, R88, R89).\n\n**First 3 paths confirmed:**\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T71.md`\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T72.md`\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T73.md`\n\n**Summary of coverage:**\n- **T71–T76** (frontend): each task brief follows the prior-agent template (Goal, Spec excerpts, Inputs, Steps, Files expected, Verification, Coding-standard checks with UI-specification subsection, Definition of done). Every frontend brief honors the mandated UI-spec subsection covering i18n via `i18n.t(...)`, native `<dialog>` modals (no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`), `try/catch` with `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` (the VS Code analogue to `parseApiError + toast.error`), `vscode.window.showQuickPick` for ID inputs, sortable headers / pagination, and loading/empty/error/badge states for every interactive surface.\n- **T77** (extension UI smoke tests): VS Code test runner via `node ./out/test/runTest.js`; covers activation, view registration, and per-subview render under happy/empty/error.\n- **R78** (Phase 10 frontend review): all required headings (Spec alignment, Coding-standard alignment, UI-specification compliance walking ui-specification.md sections explicitly, i18n coverage, Findings, Verdict).\n- **R79** (Extension↔Daemon contract gate): introduces `extension/src/test/contract.test.ts`; required headings (Contract surfaces, Round-trip evidence, Schema surfaces, Findings, Verdict).\n- **T80–T84** (Phase 11 hardening tests): large-repo perf (with P99 budgets from Phases 1+2), concurrency (parallel sessions / write contention / replay-safe), recovery + corruption, partial-index + workspace-boundary (per spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties), migration + MCP schema compat regression (uses R64 shims).\n- **T85** (documentation suite): all eleven required docs from spec §Documentation Requirements.\n- **T86** (runbook + recovery playbook): quotes the `pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/` sequence from `lattice/CLAUDE.md` and documents the replay-from-snapshot procedure validated by T82.\n- **R87** (Phase 11 hardening + docs review): required headings including Doc coverage matrix (one row per required doc from spec §Documentation Requirements) and Hardening evidence.\n- **R88** (Definition-of-Done gate): all nine checklist sections from `shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md`; `## Explicit Deferrals` must be empty or list each deferral with reason+owner+target-date per the execution philosophy in `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md`.\n- **R89** (Final readiness review): required matrices (Spec coverage by phase, Measurable success criteria with evidence file paths, Risk controls in place with implementation+test evidence), plus a binding Ship verdict (PASS or BLOCK). Verification runs full `cargo test --workspace` and extension compile/lint/test.\n\nNo code in `daemon/` or `extension/` was modified. `tasks.json`, `context.md`, and `plan.md` were not modified."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a78780aeb6875bd27 (use SendMessage with to: 'a78780aeb6875bd27' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 160303\ntool_uses: 45\nduration_ms: 987098</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"1cb8777f-e882-4413-87c0-cfea5424ca22","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:49:46.880Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are writing markdown task brief files for a build harness. Do NOT implement the feature itself — just write the brief markdown files.\n\nRepo root: /home/pete/cadres/lattice\nSpec: /home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md  (READ Phases 10 and 11 + §Human Review Surface + §Documentation Requirements + §Testing Requirements + §Measurable Success Criteria + §Risks)\nContext: /home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md\nTasks JSON: /home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks.json\nShared standards: /home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md AND /home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md (READ FOR T71–T77 — frontend tasks must honor this) AND /home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md (READ FOR R88 — that review walks the entire checklist).\n\nWrite these 19 files at `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/<TASK_ID>.md`:\n\nT71, T72, T73, T74, T75, T76, T77, R78, R79, T80, T81, T82, T83, T84, T85, T86, R87, R88, R89\n\n**Use the same template structure as prior agents.**\n\nFor frontend tasks (T71–T77), the \"Coding-standard checks\" section MUST add a \"UI specification\" subsection covering:\n- All user-facing strings via `i18n.t(...)` (no inline literals).\n- All modals via native `<dialog>` (no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`).\n- All mutation handlers use `try/catch` with `parseApiError(e)` + `toast.error(...)` if applicable to extension context — for VS Code extension, use `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` where `parseApiError` does not apply.\n- All ID inputs use `SearchableSelect` (where applicable to VS Code webview UI; for tree views use `vscode.window.showQuickPick` patterns).\n- Sortable tables use `SortableHeader` + `useTableSort` (where applicable).\n- Status badges + loading + empty + error states for every interactive surface.\n\n**Task-specific guidance:**\n\n- T71 — extension review-panel scaffolding: register a new webview view contribution under existing `viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice` in `extension/package.json`. RPC bridge translates tool responses into typed UI state. Set up i18n with at least `en.json`.\n- T72 — memory inbox: list view with filters (status, scope, class), status badges (verified/unverified/in_review/stale/contradicted/superseded/expired/invalidated).\n- T73 — promotion queue + contradiction queue with accept/reject. Dialogs are native `<dialog>` per ui-specification.md.\n- T74 — stale memory view + evidence inspector + verification trigger that calls `verify_memory` (new tool from Phase 8).\n- T75 — event trace view + retrieval explanation view (shows ranking scores + reasons from Phase 4 diagnostic mode).\n- T76 — consolidation queue + indexing health + workspace graph health.\n- T77 — extension UI smoke tests via VS Code test runner (`npm test` → `node ./out/test/runTest.js`). Tests cover at least: extension activates, review panel registers, each subview renders without throwing.\n- R78 — Phase 10 review. Required headings: `## Spec alignment`, `## Coding-standard alignment`, `## UI-specification compliance` (walk the ui-specification.md sections explicitly), `## i18n coverage`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`.\n- R79 — contract gate Extension ↔ Daemon. Required headings: `## Contract surfaces`, `## Round-trip evidence`, `## Schema surfaces`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`. New file `extension/src/test/contract.test.ts`.\n- T80 — large-repo performance tests: fixtures from T66, P99 budgets from Phases 1 and 2 verified at scale.\n- T81 — concurrency tests: parallel sessions, write contention, replay-safe execution.\n- T82 — recovery + corruption: corrupted event payloads, partial snapshots, replay-from-snapshot.\n- T83 — partial-index handling + workspace-boundary tests (per spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties \"no silent broad workspace reads\").\n- T84 — migration tests + MCP schema compatibility regression (covers backward-compat shims from R64).\n- T85 — documentation suite. Required docs from spec §Documentation Requirements:\n  - successor architecture overview\n  - MCP contract reference (was T61)\n  - memory model reference\n  - event log design\n  - consolidation design\n  - retrieval/ranking design\n  - verification/freshness design\n  - operator guide\n  - migration guide from Lattice\n  - benchmark/evaluation guide\n  - extension review UI guide\n- T86 — operator runbook + recovery playbook. Includes deploy sequence: `pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/` from `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy. Includes replay-from-snapshot procedure.\n- R87 — Phase 11 review. Required headings: `## Spec alignment`, `## Coding-standard alignment`, `## Doc coverage matrix` (one row per required doc from spec §Documentation Requirements), `## Hardening evidence`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`.\n- R88 — definition-of-done gate. Required headings (one per checklist section from `shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md`):\n  - `## Workflow Completion`\n  - `## Failure Handling`\n  - `## Security, Tenancy, and Audit`\n  - `## Data Integrity and Workflow Controls`\n  - `## Observability and Recovery`\n  - `## Performance and Scale`\n  - `## Documentation and Operator Readiness`\n  - `## Verification Evidence`\n  - `## Explicit Deferrals` — this MUST be either empty (no deferrals) or list each deferral with reason + owner + target date per the execution philosophy in `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md`. No silent deferrals.\n- R89 — final readiness review. Required headings:\n  - `## Spec coverage matrix` — one row per spec §Phase, status PASS/PASS-WITH-CAVEATS/BLOCK.\n  - `## Measurable success criteria` — one row per target from spec §Measurable Success Criteria (initial targets); evidence file path.\n  - `## Risk controls in place` — one row per risk from spec §Risks; control implementation evidence.\n  - `## Ship verdict` — PASS or BLOCK.\n\nR89 verification includes a full `cargo test --workspace` + extension compile/lint/test.\n\nRead the spec yourself for exact text. Quote it. Read ui-specification.md and definition-of-done-checklist.md sections relevant to the tasks you write.\n\nFor each task: 5-10 concrete steps, exact file paths from tasks.json, exact verification commands from tasks.json.\n\nWrite all 19 files, then report file count + first 3 paths confirmed. Do NOT modify tasks.json, context.md, or plan.md. Do NOT implement any code in daemon/ or extension/.","agentId":"a78780aeb6875bd27","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 19 task brief files written successfully.\n\n**File count:** 19 (T71, T72, T73, T74, T75, T76, T77, R78, R79, T80, T81, T82, T83, T84, T85, T86, R87, R88, R89).\n\n**First 3 paths confirmed:**\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T71.md`\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T72.md`\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T73.md`\n\n**Summary of coverage:**\n- **T71–T76** (frontend): each task brief follows the prior-agent template (Goal, Spec excerpts, Inputs, Steps, Files expected, Verification, Coding-standard checks with UI-specification subsection, Definition of done). Every frontend brief honors the mandated UI-spec subsection covering i18n via `i18n.t(...)`, native `<dialog>` modals (no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`), `try/catch` with `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` (the VS Code analogue to `parseApiError + toast.error`), `vscode.window.showQuickPick` for ID inputs, sortable headers / pagination, and loading/empty/error/badge states for every interactive surface.\n- **T77** (extension UI smoke tests): VS Code test runner via `node ./out/test/runTest.js`; covers activation, view registration, and per-subview render under happy/empty/error.\n- **R78** (Phase 10 frontend review): all required headings (Spec alignment, Coding-standard alignment, UI-specification compliance walking ui-specification.md sections explicitly, i18n coverage, Findings, Verdict).\n- **R79** (Extension↔Daemon contract gate): introduces `extension/src/test/contract.test.ts`; required headings (Contract surfaces, Round-trip evidence, Schema surfaces, Findings, Verdict).\n- **T80–T84** (Phase 11 hardening tests): large-repo perf (with P99 budgets from Phases 1+2), concurrency (parallel sessions / write contention / replay-safe), recovery + corruption, partial-index + workspace-boundary (per spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties), migration + MCP schema compat regression (uses R64 shims).\n- **T85** (documentation suite): all eleven required docs from spec §Documentation Requirements.\n- **T86** (runbook + recovery playbook): quotes the `pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/` sequence from `lattice/CLAUDE.md` and documents the replay-from-snapshot procedure validated by T82.\n- **R87** (Phase 11 hardening + docs review): required headings including Doc coverage matrix (one row per required doc from spec §Documentation Requirements) and Hardening evidence.\n- **R88** (Definition-of-Done gate): all nine checklist sections from `shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md`; `## Explicit Deferrals` must be empty or list each deferral with reason+owner+target-date per the execution philosophy in `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md`.\n- **R89** (Final readiness review): required matrices (Spec coverage by phase, Measurable success criteria with evidence file paths, Risk controls in place with implementation+test evidence), plus a binding Ship verdict (PASS or BLOCK). Verification runs full `cargo test --workspace` and extension compile/lint/test.\n\nNo code in `daemon/` or `extension/` was modified. `tasks.json`, `context.md`, and `plan.md` were not modified."}],"totalDurationMs":987098,"totalTokens":160303,"totalToolUseCount":45,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":825,"cache_read_input_tokens":158112,"output_tokens":1365,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":825},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":1365,"cache_read_input_tokens":158112,"cache_creation_input_tokens":825,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":825,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":15,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":9,"editFileCount":19,"linesAdded":1605,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
