n: corrupted event payloads, partial snapshots, replay-from-snapshot.\n- T83 — partial-index handling + workspace-boundary tests (per spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties \"no silent broad workspace reads\").\n- T84 — migration tests + MCP schema compatibility regression (covers backward-compat shims from R64).\n- T85 — documentation suite. Required docs from spec §Documentation Requirements:\n  - successor architecture overview\n  - MCP contract reference (was T61)\n  - memory model reference\n  - event log design\n  - consolidation design\n  - retrieval/ranking design\n  - verification/freshness design\n  - operator guide\n  - migration guide from Lattice\n  - benchmark/evaluation guide\n  - extension review UI guide\n- T86 — operator runbook + recovery playbook. Includes deploy sequence: `pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/` from `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy. Includes replay-from-snapshot procedure.\n- R87 — Phase 11 review. Required headings: `## Spec alignment`, `## Coding-standard alignment`, `## Doc coverage matrix` (one row per required doc from spec §Documentation Requirements), `## Hardening evidence`, `## Findings`, `## Verdict`.\n- R88 — definition-of-done gate. Required headings (one per checklist section from `shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md`):\n  - `## Workflow Completion`\n  - `## Failure Handling`\n  - `## Security, Tenancy, and Audit`\n  - `## Data Integrity and Workflow Controls`\n  - `## Observability and Recovery`\n  - `## Performance and Scale`\n  - `## Documentation and Operator Readiness`\n  - `## Verification Evidence`\n  - `## Explicit Deferrals` — this MUST be either empty (no deferrals) or list each deferral with reason + owner + target date per the execution philosophy in `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md`. No silent deferrals.\n- R89 — final readiness review. Required headings:\n  - `## Spec coverage matrix` — one row per spec §Phase, status PASS/PASS-WITH-CAVEATS/BLOCK.\n  - `## Measurable success criteria` — one row per target from spec §Measurable Success Criteria (initial targets); evidence file path.\n  - `## Risk controls in place` — one row per risk from spec §Risks; control implementation evidence.\n  - `## Ship verdict` — PASS or BLOCK.\n\nR89 verification includes a full `cargo test --workspace` + extension compile/lint/test.\n\nRead the spec yourself for exact text. Quote it. Read ui-specification.md and definition-of-done-checklist.md sections relevant to the tasks you write.\n\nFor each task: 5-10 concrete steps, exact file paths from tasks.json, exact verification commands from tasks.json.\n\nWrite all 19 files, then report file count + first 3 paths confirmed. Do NOT modify tasks.json, context.md, or plan.md. Do NOT implement any code in daemon/ or extension/.","agentId":"a78780aeb6875bd27","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All 19 task brief files written successfully.\n\n**File count:** 19 (T71, T72, T73, T74, T75, T76, T77, R78, R79, T80, T81, T82, T83, T84, T85, T86, R87, R88, R89).\n\n**First 3 paths confirmed:**\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T71.md`\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T72.md`\n- `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T73.md`\n\n**Summary of coverage:**\n- **T71–T76** (frontend): each task brief follows the prior-agent template (Goal, Spec excerpts, Inputs, Steps, Files expected, Verification, Coding-standard checks with UI-specification subsection, Definition of done). Every frontend brief honors the mandated UI-spec subsection covering i18n via `i18n.t(...)`, native `<dialog>` modals (no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`), `try/catch` with `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` (the VS Code analogue to `parseApiError + toast.error`), `vscode.window.showQuickPick` for ID inputs, sortable headers / pagination, and loading/empty/error/badge states for every interactive surface.\n- **T77** (extension UI smoke tests): VS Code test runner via `node ./out/test/runTest.js`; covers activation, view registration, and per-subview render under happy/empty/error.\n- **R78** (Phase 10 frontend review): all required headings (Spec alignment, Coding-standard alignment, UI-specification compliance walking ui-specification.md sections explicitly, i18n coverage, Findings, Verdict).\n- **R79** (Extension↔Daemon contract gate): introduces `extension/src/test/contract.test.ts`; required headings (Contract surfaces, Round-trip evidence, Schema surfaces, Findings, Verdict).\n- **T80–T84** (Phase 11 hardening tests): large-repo perf (with P99 budgets from Phases 1+2), concurrency (parallel sessions / write contention / replay-safe), recovery + corruption, partial-index + workspace-boundary (per spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties), migration + MCP schema compat regression (uses R64 shims).\n- **T85** (documentation suite): all eleven required docs from spec §Documentation Requirements.\n- **T86** (runbook + recovery playbook): quotes the `pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/` sequence from `lattice/CLAUDE.md` and documents the replay-from-snapshot procedure validated by T82.\n- **R87** (Phase 11 hardening + docs review): required headings including Doc coverage matrix (one row per required doc from spec §Documentation Requirements) and Hardening evidence.\n- **R88** (Definition-of-Done gate): all nine checklist sections from `shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md`; `## Explicit Deferrals` must be empty or list each deferral with reason+owner+target-date per the execution philosophy in `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md`.\n- **R89** (Final readiness review): required matrices (Spec coverage by phase, Measurable success criteria with evidence file paths, Risk controls in place with implementation+test evidence), plus a binding Ship verdict (PASS or BLOCK). Verification runs full `cargo test --workspace` and extension compile/lint/test.\n\nNo code in `daemon/` or `extension/` was modified. `tasks.json`, `context.md`, and `plan.md` were not modified."}],"totalDurationMs":987098,"totalTokens":160303,"totalToolUseCount":45,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":825,"cache_read_input_tokens":158112,"output_tokens":1365,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":825},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":1365,"cache_read_input_tokens":158112,"cache_creation_input_tokens":825,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":825,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":15,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":9,"editFileCount":19,"linesAdded":1605,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T71.log:8006:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/R18.log:69:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YDddTfUMWEqFt9sDKfKEEo","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tuse serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\n2\tuse std::str::FromStr;\n3\t\n4\tuse crate::events::DocSectionId;\n5\tuse crate::identity::{ContextHandleId, EventId, FileId, MemoryId, SymbolId};\n6\t\n7\t/// Event kinds defined by `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md`\n8\t/// `### 3. Event Log`:\n9\t/// `AssistantTaskStarted`, `ToolCalled`, `ToolResult`, `ContextBundleReturned`,\n10\t/// `MemoryRetrieved`, `MemoryExpanded`, `PlanCreated`, `FileRead`, `PatchApplied`,\n11\t/// `TestRunStarted`, `TestRunCompleted`, `DiagnosticObserved`, `UserCorrection`,\n12\t/// `UserPreferenceObserved`, `WorkflowSucceeded`, `WorkflowFailed`, `MemoryCreated`,\n13\t/// `MemoryUpdated`, `MemoryInvalidated`, `MemoryConsolidated`.\n14\t#[derive(Serialize, Deserialize, Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Hash)]\n15\t#[serde(rename_all = \"snake_case\")]\n16\tpub enum EventKind {\n17\t    AssistantTaskStarted,\n18\t    ToolCalled,\n19\t    ToolResult,\n20\t    ContextBundleReturned,\n21\t    MemoryRetrieved,\n22\t    MemoryExpanded,\n23\t    PlanCreated,\n24\t    FileRead,\n25\t    PatchApplied,\n26\t    TestRunStarted,\n27\t    TestRunCompleted,\n28\t    DiagnosticObserved,\n29\t    UserCorrection,\n30\t    UserPreferenceObserved,\n31\t    WorkflowSucceeded,\n32\t    WorkflowFailed,\n33\t    MemoryCreated,\n34\t    MemoryUpdated,\n35\t    MemoryInvalidated,\n36\t    MemoryConsolidated,\n37\t}\n38\t\n39\timpl EventKind {\n40\t    /// Return the canonical snake_case wire name used in storage.\n41\t    pub fn as_str(self) -> &'static str {\n42\t        match self {\n43\t            EventKind::AssistantTaskStarted => \"assistant_task_started\",\n44\t            EventKind::ToolCalled => \"tool_called\",\n45\t            EventKind::ToolResult => \"tool_result\",\n46\t            EventKind::ContextBundleReturned => \"context_bundle_returned\",\n47\t            EventKind::MemoryRetrieved => \"memory_retrieved\",\n48\t            EventKind::MemoryExpanded => \"memory_expanded\",\n49\t            EventKind::PlanCreated => \"plan_created\",\n50\t            EventKind::FileRead => \"file_read\",\n51\t            EventKind::PatchApplied => \"patch_applied\",\n52\t            EventKind::TestRunStarted => \"test_run_started\",\n53\t            EventKind::TestRunCompleted => \"test_run_completed\",\n54\t            EventKind::DiagnosticObserved => \"diagnostic_observed\",\n55\t            EventKind::UserCorrection => \"user_correction\",\n56\t            EventKind::UserPreferenceObserved => \"user_preference_observed\",\n57\t            EventKind::WorkflowSucceeded => \"workflow_succeeded\",\n58\t            EventKind::WorkflowFailed => \"workflow_failed\",\n59\t            EventKind::MemoryCreated => \"memory_created\",\n60\t            EventKind::MemoryUpdated => \"memory_updated\",\n61\t            EventKind::MemoryInvalidated => \"memory_invalidated\",\n62\t            EventKind::MemoryConsolidated => \"memory_consolidated\",\n63\t        }\n64\t    }\n65\t}\n66\t\n67\timpl FromStr for EventKind {\n68\t    type Err = String;\n69\t\n70\t    fn from_str(value: &str) -> Result<Self, Self::Err> {\n71\t        match value {\n72\t            \"assistant_task_started\" => Ok(EventKind::AssistantTaskStarted),\n73\t            \"tool_called\" => Ok(EventKind::ToolCalled),\n74\t            \"tool_result\" => Ok(EventKind::ToolResult),\n75\t            \"context_bundle_returned\" => Ok(EventKind::ContextBundleReturned),\n76\t            \"memory_retrieved\" => Ok(EventKind::MemoryRetrieved),\n77\t            \"memory_expanded\" => Ok(EventKind::MemoryExpanded),\n78\t            \"plan_created\" => Ok(EventKind::PlanCreated),\n79\t            \"file_read\" => Ok(EventKind::FileRead),\n80\t            \"patch_applied\" => Ok(EventKind::PatchApplied),\n81\t            \"test_run_started\" => Ok(EventKind::TestRunStarted),\n82\t            \"test_run_completed\" => Ok(EventKind::TestRunCompleted),\n83\t            \"diagnostic_observed\" => Ok(EventKind::DiagnosticObserved),\n84\t            \"user_correction\" => Ok(EventKind::UserCorrection),\n85\t            \"user_preference_observed\" => Ok(EventKind::UserPreferenceObserved),\n86\t            \"workflow_succeeded\" => Ok(EventKind::WorkflowSucceeded),\n87\t            \"workflow_failed\" => Ok(EventKind::WorkflowFailed),\n88\t            \"memory_created\" => Ok(EventKind::MemoryCreated),\n89\t            \"memory_updated\" => Ok(EventKind::MemoryUpdated),\n90\t            \"memory_invalidated\" => Ok(EventKind::MemoryInvalidated),\n91\t            \"memory_consolidated\" => Ok(EventKind::MemoryConsolidated),\n92\t            other => Err(format!(\"unknown event kind `{other}`\")),\n93\t        }\n94\t    }\n95\t}\n96\t\n97\t/// Tool result status recorded in event payloads.\n98\t#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n99\t#[serde(rename_all = \"snake_case\")]\n100\tpub enum ToolResultStatus {\n101\t    Succeeded,\n102\t    Failed,\n103\t    Partial,\n104\t}\n105\t\n106\t/// Test run terminal status recorded in event payloads.\n107\t#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n108\t#[serde(rename_all = \"snake_case\")]\n109\tpub enum TestRunStatus {\n110\t    Passed,\n111\t    Failed,\n112\t    Cancelled,\n113\t}\n114\t\n115\t/// Diagnostic severity normalized for the event stream.\n116\t#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n117\t#[serde(rename_all = \"snake_case\")]\n118\tpub enum DiagnosticSeverity {\n119\t    Info,\n120\t    Warning,\n121\t    Error,\n122\t}\n123\t\n124\t/// Payload for `assistant_task_started`.\n125\t#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n126\tpub struct AssistantTaskStartedPayload {\n127\t    /// Context handle created for the task, when available.\n128\t    pub context_handle_id: Option<ContextHandleId>,\n129\t    /// Prior events used to seed the task context.\n130\t    pub seed_event_ids: Vec<EventId>,\n131\t    /// Existing memories surfaced before the task started.\n132\t    pub initial_memory_ids: Vec<MemoryId>,\n133\t    /// Compact objective for the task start event.\n134\t    pub objective: String,\n135\t}\n136\t\n137\t/// Payload for `tool_called`.\n138\t#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n139\tpub struct ToolCalledPayload {\n140\t    /// Unique call id for matching request and result events.\n141\t    pub call_id: String,\n142\t    /// Tool name invoked by the assistant or daemon.\n143\t    pub tool_name: String,\n144\t    /// Context handle supplied to the tool call.\n145\t    pub context_handle_id: Option<ContextHandleId>,\n146\t    /// Event that requested the tool call, when chained.\n147\t    pub source_event_id: Option<EventId>,\n148\t    /// Compact summary of the call input.\n149\t    pub input_summary: String,\n150\t}\n151\t\n152\t/// Payload for `tool_result`.\n153\t#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n154\tpub struct ToolResultPayload {\n155\t    /// Tool call id that this result satisfies.\n156\t    pub call_id: String,\n157\t    /// Tool name that produced the result.\n158\t    pub tool_name: String,\n159\t    /// Terminal status for the tool call.\n160\t    pub status: ToolResultStatus,\n161\t    /// Event id of the originating tool call.\n162\t    pub tool_call_event_id: Option<EventId>,\n163\t    /// Context handle produced by the tool, when any.\n164\t    pub output_context_handle_id: Option<ContextHandleId>,\n165\t    /// Memories materialized directly by the tool result.\n166\t    pub created_memory_ids: Vec<MemoryId>,\n167\t    /// Compact summary of the tool output.\n168\t    pub output_summary: String,\n169\t}\n170\t\n171\t/// Payload for `context_bundle_returned`.\n172\t#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n173\tpub struct ContextBundleReturnedPayload {\n174\t    /// Stable handle for the returned bundle.\n175\t    pub context_handle_id: ContextHandleId,\n176\t    /// Event that requested the bundle.\n177\t    pub source_event_id: Option<EventId>,\n178\t    /// Files included in the returned bundle.\n179\t    pub file_ids: Vec<FileId>,\n180\t    /// Symbols included in the returned bundle.\n181\t    pub symbol_ids: Vec<SymbolId>,\n182\t    /// Document sections included in the bundle.\n183\t    pub doc_section_ids: Vec<DocSectionId>,\n184\t    /// Memories included in the bundle.\n185\t    pub memory_ids: Vec<MemoryId>,\n186\t    /// Approximate token budget of the returned bundle.\n187\t    pub token_estimate: u32,\n188\t}\n189\t\n190\t/// Payload for `memory_retrieved`.\n191\t#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n192\tpub struct MemoryRetrievedPayload {\n193\t    /// Query or task string used to retrieve memories.\n194\t    pub retrieval_query: String,\n195\t    /// Context handle associated with the retrieval.\n196\t    pub context_handle_id: Option<ContextHandleId>,\n197\t    /// Memories returned to the assistant.\n198\t    pub memory_ids: Vec<MemoryId>,\n199\t    /// Evidence events cited by the retrieval layer.\n200\t    pub supporting_event_ids: Vec<EventId>,\n201\t}\n202\t\n203\t/// Payload for `memory_expanded`.\n204\t#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n205\tpub struct MemoryExpandedPayload {\n206\t    /// Memory whose neighborhood was expanded.\n207\t    pub memory_id: MemoryId,\n208\t    /// Event that requested the expansion.\n209\t    pub source_event_id: Option<EventId>,\n210\t    /// Additional memories surfaced by expansion.\n211\t    pub linked_memory_ids: Vec<MemoryId>,\n212\t    /// Symbols surfaced by expansion.\n213\t    pub linked_symbol_ids: Vec<SymbolId>,\n214\t    /// Document sections surfaced by expansion.\n215\t    pub linked_doc_section_ids: Vec<DocSectionId>,\n216\t}\n217\t\n218\t/// Payload for `plan_created`.\n219\t#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n220\tpub struct PlanCreatedPayload {\n221\t    /// Context handle attached to the plan output.\n222\t    pub context_handle_id: Option<ContextHandleId>,\n223\t    /// Event that triggered plan creation.\n224\t    pub source_event_id: Option<EventId>,\n225\t    /// Memory ids cited while creating the plan.\n226\t    pub memory_ids: Vec<MemoryId>,\n227\t    /// Number of plan steps produced.\n228\t    pub step_count: u32,\n229\t    /// Compact summary of the plan body.\n230\t    pub plan_summary: String,\n231\t}\n232\t\n233\t/// Payload for `file_read`.\n234\t#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n235\tpub struct FileReadPayload {\n236\t    /// File read by the assistant, daemon, or tool.\n237\t    pub file_id: FileId,\n238\t    /// Event that triggered the file read.\n239\t    pub source_event_id: Option<EventId>,\n240\t    /// Optional first byte offset included in the read.\n241\t    pub byte_start: Option<u32>,\n242\t    /// Optional exclusive end byte offset included in the read.\n243\t    pub byte_end: Option<u32>,\n244\t    /// Compact purpose statement for the read.\n245\t    pub reason: String,\n246\t}\n247\t\n248\t/// Payload for `patch_applied`.\n249\t#[derive(Clone, Debug, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n250\tpub struct PatchAppliedPayload {\n251\t    /// Stable patch or edit operation id.\n252\t    pub patch_id: String,\n253\t    /// Event that produced the patch.\n254\t    pub source_event_id: Option<EventId>,\n255\t    /// Files changed by the patch.\n256\t    pub file_ids: Vec<FileId>,\n257\t    /// Symbols changed by the patch.\n258\t    pub symbol_ids: Vec<SymbolId>,\n259\t    /// Number of inserted lines in the patch.\n260\t    pub lines_added: u32,\n261\t    /// Number of removed lines in the patch.\n262\t    pub lines_removed: u32,\n263\t}\n264\t\n265\t/// Payload for `test_run_started`.\n266\t#[derive(Clone, Debug,t) -> Vec<RankedCandidate>` that calls each evaluator, sums weighted scores, fills `inclusion_reason` with a compact summary in `Compact` mode and the full per-signal list in `Diagnostic` mode, then sorts descending.\n5. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/diagnostic.rs`: the public API for diagnostic mode. Exposes `RankingDiagnostics { intent, anchors, candidates_per_source, ranked: Vec<RankedCandidate>, weights, budget_exhaustion_flags }` plus serde derivation, so T31 (shaper) and T36 (MCP working-memory inspector) can hand the diagnostics straight to the caller without further transformation.\n6. Honor spec §Stale Memory Leakage by ensuring `verification_status` and `contradiction_or_supersession_state` apply a hard penalty (not just a soft weight) when status is `stale`, `contradicted`, or `superseded`, so these candidates cannot rank above trusted ones except when diagnostics explicitly request them. The penalty value and policy live in `ScoringWeights` with inline documentation citing `## Stale Memory Leakage`.\n7. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/scoring_tests.rs` covering: each of the thirteen signals is evaluated for a representative candidate; signal absence yields a `0.0` score and a documented reason (no panic); stale/contradicted candidates rank below trusted candidates with the same raw match; diagnostic mode round-trips signal scores and reasons through serde; compact mode produces a non-empty single-line inclusion reason; weight overrides change the ranking deterministically; user-preference compatibility raises the score of candidates matching a stored preference and lowers candidates conflicting with one.\n8. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and the verification test command; fix any clippy warnings before stopping.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/scoring.rs` — signal evaluators, weights, orchestrator.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/diagnostic.rs` — diagnostics types and serde, public API for diagnostic mode.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/scoring_tests.rs` — in-tree tests for every signal, both modes, and the stale-memory hard penalty.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/scoring.rs` — confirms the scorer module exists at the expected path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/diagnostic.rs` — confirms the diagnostics module exists at the expected path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib retrieval_v1::scoring_tests` — runs the in-tree tests and asserts every signal and both modes are covered.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file. If `scoring.rs` approaches the cap, split signal evaluators into `scoring/` submodules grouped by data dependency (graph signals, memory signals, history signals).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. Each signal evaluator is its own function.\n- Schema parity: not applicable (no SQL in this task, but field reads from `memories` use the canonical structs).\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Single source of truth: weights, defaults, and signal-kind enum are defined once and consumed everywhere; no parallel weight tables in callers.\n- No broken windows: dead imports removed, every public name expresses intent; no silent `.unwrap()` on Optional signal data.\n- Doc citation: the signal table at the top of `scoring.rs` cites `## 7. Retrieval Engine`; the stale-memory hard penalty comment cites `## Stale Memory Leakage` per `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every one of the thirteen ranking signals from spec §Retrieval Engine is implemented as a signal evaluator with a test.\n- [ ] Diagnostic mode emits both per-signal scores and reasons; compact mode emits a single-line inclusion reason — both verified by tests.\n- [ ] Stale/contradicted/superseded candidates cannot rank above trusted candidates with the same raw match (regression test pinned).\n- [ ] Diagnostics struct is exported and exercised by a test, ready to be consumed by T31 (shaper) and T36 (working-memory inspector).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3306,"cache_read_input_tokens":52740,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3306,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":58,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019RjXwCuf57BAKn88mWkE24","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"51d549ac-52ac-4ed7-9cd1-26fc808d3401","request_id":"req_011Cb7MvMPAgod1NwX41bo3F","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 4+5 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T71.log:8007:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T41.log:1959:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2726:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:711:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_016MBNff4Vmm8f1myrXiWNYC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WwRjPQsgfzTC4J4X1QNmPX","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T59.md","content":"# T59 — Phase 8 — New memory tools: verify_memory + explain_memory (unified) + list_memory_conflicts\n\n**Phase:** 8\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** R55\n**Spec anchor:** [§MCP Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#9-mcp-surface)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task introduces the unified `verify_explain_memory` MCP tool (collapsing the spec's `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` per §MCP Surface \"Tool surface discipline\") and the new `list_memory_conflicts` tool. The unified tool runs verification checks against a memory and returns both the resulting status (`verified` | `unverified` | `in_review` | `stale` | `contradicted` | `superseded` | `expired` | `invalidated`) and a structured explanation of why each linked file/symbol/doc/test contributed to that status. `list_memory_conflicts` surfaces all contradiction and supersession edges anchored on a target memory or graph node so an operator can review them. Both tools are required by spec §MCP Surface and are the trust surface for the verification engine — without them, the Phase 7 verifier produces statuses that no assistant or operator can interrogate.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"New or redesigned memory tools: …`verify_memory`, `explain_memory`, `list_memory_conflicts`…\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n> \"Tool surface discipline: …audit whether `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` can be unified. The goal is the smallest surface that covers all assistant workflows. Consolidate before stabilizing the MCP contract.\" (spec §MCP Surface)\n\n> \"Verification outputs: `verified`, `unverified`, `in_review`, `stale`, `contradicted`, `superseded`, `expired`, `invalidated`.\" (spec §Verification Engine)\n\n> \"Every memory-bearing response should identify: why the memory was included; evidence strength; verification status; freshness status; contradiction/supersession state; scope; expansion target.\" (spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R55 (Phase 7 verification engine review) — `verify_explain_memory` is the MCP surface for the Phase 7 verifier; its outputs must match the Phase 7 status taxonomy exactly.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/verification/` — Phase 7 verifier (existence, span, scope, expiry checks; incremental verification jobs).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — Phase 3 memory store + links + evidence + accesses + scores.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` — Phase 2 event writer (emits `MemoryRetrieved`, `MemoryInvalidated`).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — MCP tool registration.\n  - Phase 1 identity types.\n  - T58 shared `MemoryRecord` type.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Hard limits, §Single source of truth, §No broken windows, §Schema parity, §Observability; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references; `AGENTS.md` backward-compatibility discipline.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §MCP Surface and §Verification Engine in full. Quote the \"Tool surface discipline\" paragraph verbatim into a doc comment on `memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` so the rationale for unifying `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` is preserved at the call site.\n2. Implement `verify_explain_memory` in `memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` as the unified tool. Accept `memory_id: MemoryId` plus optional `mode: \"verify\" | \"explain\" | \"verify_and_explain\"` (default `verify_and_explain`). Internally run the Phase 7 verifier on the memory, then construct an `ExplainReport` that itemizes each verification check (linked file exists, linked symbol exists, cited doc exists, linked test exists, evidence span still matches, contradiction state coherent, scope leak absent, time-bound expiry honored) and the outcome per check. Return a `VerifyExplainResponse` with `status: VerificationStatus`, `checks: Vec<CheckResult>` (each carrying `kind`, `target`, `outcome`, `evidence_ref`), `confidence_delta`, and an `expansion_handle` so the caller can pull richer context.\n3. Mode semantics: `verify` returns only the status + a minimal summary; `explain` skips re-running checks and returns the last persisted `ExplainReport`; `verify_and_explain` runs verification and returns the freshly-derived `ExplainReport`. Default is `verify_and_explain` because spec §Verification Engine and §MCP Tool Contract Principles together require fresh, explainable status on every memory surface.\n4. Implement `list_memory_conflicts` in `memory_v2/list_memory_conflicts.rs`. Accept `anchor: MemoryId | FileId | SymbolId | DocSectionId`, traverse `contradicts` and `supersedes` link types from Phase 3 memory_links, and return a paginated list of `ConflictRecord`s each carrying `source`, `target`, `link_type` (`contradicts` | `supersedes`), `link_strength`, `created_by`, `created_at`, `link_verification_status`, and `reason`. Honor scope filters per spec §Risks \"Scope Leakage.\"\n5. Both tools must emit appropriate Phase 2 events: `verify_explain_memory` emits `MemoryRetrieved` plus `MemoryInvalidated` when status transitions away from `verified`; `list_memory_conflicts` emits `MemoryRetrieved` for each surfaced conflict.\n6. Both tools must support compact + full + diagnostic render modes per spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles. Compact mode for `verify_explain_memory` returns status + one-line per failed check; diagnostic mode returns the full check trace.\n7. Register the two tools in `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs`. Per the T02 compatibility policy, the spec's nominal `verify_memory` and `explain_memory` names continue to respond as deprecation shims that forward to `verify_explain_memory` with the appropriate `mode`. The shims emit a deprecation warning in the response.\n8. In `memory_v2/verify_explain_tests.rs`, write tests that: (a) round-trip each tool's request/response through serde; (b) assert `verify_explain_memory` returns the exact Phase 7 status taxonomy and never invents a new status string; (c) assert every check result carries an evidence reference; (d) assert a memory whose linked symbol was deleted transitions to `stale` with a `linked_symbol_missing` check failure; (e) assert `list_memory_conflicts` returns both `contradicts` and `supersedes` edges with correct directionality; (f) assert scope-leak negative tests; (g) assert the `verify_memory` and `explain_memory` deprecation shims continue to respond and forward correctly.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` — unified verify+explain tool with `mode` parameter, full check trace, and expansion handle, per spec §MCP Surface tool-surface discipline.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/list_memory_conflicts.rs` — `list_memory_conflicts` traversing contradiction + supersession links with scope-aware pagination.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/verify_explain_tests.rs` — unit + scope-leak negative tests covering schema, status taxonomy parity with Phase 7, evidence-reference completeness, conflict directionality, and shim forwarding.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/verify_explain_memory.rs` — confirms the unified verify+explain file exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/list_memory_conflicts.rs` — confirms the `list_memory_conflicts` file exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::memory_v2::verify_explain_tests` — every verify/explain/conflicts test passes (schema, status parity, evidence completeness, conflict directionality, scope-leak negatives, shim forwarding).\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: `VerifyExplainResponse`, `CheckResult`, `ConflictRecord` derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Debug`, `Clone`; every public field has a doc comment; the `mode` enum is serde-tagged with snake_case wire names; the `VerificationStatus` enum reuses the Phase 7 type rather than declaring a parallel one.\n- No silent error handling — every verifier failure returns a typed error; scope-leak rejections are explicit errors, not empty results.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`CheckResult::evidence_ref`, not `CheckResult::ref_str`).\n- For SQLite: all queries go through Phase 3 store and Phase 7 verifier APIs; no raw SQL in the RPC layer.\n- Observability: each tool emits `tracing::info!` spans with `tool`, `memory_id`, `scope`, `status`, `check_count` fields.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## MCP Surface`, `## Verification Engine`, and `## MCP Tool Contract Principles` in module doc comments.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: the unified `verify_explain_memory` ships in this task; the spec's two-tool API is collapsed before MCP contract stabilization per §MCP Surface.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed; the `verify_memory` + `explain_memory` deprecation shims continue to respond per the T02 compatibility policy.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4764,"cache_read_input_tokens":68448,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4764,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":59,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"unavailable"}},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"6925f9b8-658b-4628-9f96-5512a5babaa4","request_id":"req_011Cb7MzXVz1xvApfw62RZzy","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T71.log:8008:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T41.log:1965:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2732:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:755:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_017cwZPiuMEuGQCXdSJKKNBF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JyKU6KBLzr4ubAFG9yoFws","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T34.md","content":"# T34 — Phase 5 — Working memory state model + working_memory_checkpoints table\n\n**Phase:** 5\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R33\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 5: Working Memory](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-5-working-memory), [§5. Working Memory](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#5-working-memory), [§Storage Design](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers the per-task, short-lived working-memory state model the spec mandates in §5 — making context management explicit instead of implicit prompt accumulation — and pins it to a versioned `working_memory_checkpoints` SQLite table so checkpoints can be restored. The state model contains exactly the eleven fields the spec enumerates; the checkpoint schema preserves the full state along with timestamps and a checkpoint name so T35's `checkpoint` operation has a target to write into and T37's restore tests have a target to restore from.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Working memory is per-task and short-lived. It should be explicit rather than implicit prompt accumulation. Working memory contains: task statement; interpreted intent; active files and symbols; active hypotheses; active failures; current plan; selected memories; excluded memories and reasons; budget decisions; unresolved questions; verification status.\" (spec §5. Working Memory)\n\n> \"Phase 5: Working Memory — Deliverables: explicit per-task working memory state; operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint; MCP surface for inspecting current working memory; automatic event capture for included and excluded context; tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore.\" (spec §Phase 5: Working Memory)\n\n> \"Definition of done: workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded; context management is no longer just prompt accumulation.\" (spec §Phase 5: Working Memory)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - R33 verdict — Phase 4 retrieval is complete; `RetrievalBundle` / `BundleResult` are stable and can be embedded under `selected_memories`/`excluded_memories`.\n  - T27 `IntentClassification` — populates `interpreted_intent`.\n  - T28 `ResolvedAnchor` set — populates `active_files_and_symbols` seeds.\n  - T31 `RetrievalBundle` — supplies `selected_memories` and `excluded_memories` (with reasons) verbatim per the Phase 4 forward-compatibility contract.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` — SQLite connection conventions, migration helpers.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — for the `verification_status` enum reused under \"verification status\".\n  - Phase 1 identity (`IdentityResolver`) — every reference inside working memory uses a stable identity.\n- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, schema parity); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` (Markdown heading references; project build commands).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Re-read spec §5. Working Memory and §Phase 5: Working Memory in full; copy the eleven-field state list verbatim into a doc comment at the top of `state.rs`.\n2. Create the module skeleton: `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/mod.rs` re-exports `state`, `operations` (T35), `event_hooks` (T36) and registers the module with `lib.rs`.\n3. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/state.rs` with `WorkingMemoryState { task_statement: String, interpreted_intent: IntentClassification, active_files: BTreeSet<FileIdentity>, active_symbols: BTreeSet<SymbolIdentity>, active_hypotheses: Vec<Hypothesis>, active_failures: Vec<FailureRecord>, current_plan: Option<PlanRef>, selected_memories: Vec<BundleResult>, excluded_memories: Vec<ExcludedMemory>, budget_decisions: BudgetDecisions, unresolved_questions: Vec<String>, verification_status: WorkingMemoryVerification }`. Field names mirror the spec exactly. `ExcludedMemory { result: BundleResult, exclusion_reason: String }` makes the \"and reasons\" half of the spec phrase a type-system requirement, not a convention.\n4. Define supporting types in the same module: `Hypothesis { text, evidence_refs, confidence }`, `FailureRecord { kind, message, observed_at, evidence_refs }`, `PlanRef { plan_id, version }`, `BudgetDecisions { token_cap, dropped_count, truncated_results, pinned_identities }`, `WorkingMemoryVerification { last_verified_at, status: enum, notes }`.\n5. Author the schema SQL in `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/schema.sql` for the `working_memory_checkpoints` table: `(checkpoint_id INTEGER PK, workspace_id TEXT NOT NULL, session_id TEXT NOT NULL, task_id TEXT NOT NULL, checkpoint_name TEXT NOT NULL, created_at INTEGER NOT NULL, state_version INTEGER NOT NULL, state_json TEXT NOT NULL, state_hash TEXT NOT NULL)` plus indexes on `(workspace_id, session_id, task_id, created_at DESC)` and `(state_hash)`. `state_version` is bumped whenever `WorkingMemoryState` shape changes; migrations are rejected at load time if `state_version` is unknown.\n6. Wire the migration into the existing storage bootstrap: `working_memory_checkpoints` lives in the existing memory DB (`.lattice/memories.db`) so it shares the session/task identity referenced elsewhere. Document this placement decision inline with `## Storage Design` citation.\n7. Implement `save_checkpoint(state: &WorkingMemoryState, name: &str, conn: &Connection) -> Result<CheckpointId>` and `load_checkpoint(id: CheckpointId, conn: &Connection) -> Result<WorkingMemoryState>` in `state.rs`. Serialization uses serde JSON; `state_hash` is a deterministic SHA-256 over the canonical-JSON form so restore can be verified bit-for-bit.\n8. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/state_tests.rs` covering: default-constructed state has the eleven required fields; serde round-trips the state without data loss; `save_checkpoint` followed by `load_checkpoint` returns a byte-identical `WorkingMemoryState`; `state_hash` matches between save and load; loading a checkpoint with an unknown `state_version` is rejected with a clear error; `excluded_memories` cannot be constructed without an `exclusion_reason` (type-system enforced).\n9. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and the verification test command; fix any clippy warnings before stopping.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/mod.rs` — module skeleton re-exporting state, operations, event hooks.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/state.rs` — `WorkingMemoryState` + supporting types + save/load checkpoint API.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/schema.sql` — `working_memory_checkpoints` table DDL and indexes.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/state_tests.rs` — in-tree tests for shape, serde round-trip, checkpoint save/load, state-version rejection.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/mod.rs`\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/state.rs`\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib working_memory::state_tests`\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. Save/load are each their own function; serialization helpers extracted if `state.rs` approaches the cap.\n- Schema parity: every column in `working_memory_checkpoints` has a corresponding `WorkingMemoryState` field or wrapper-record field; the `state_json` column mirrors the serde shape exactly. Schema doc and Rust struct are updated in the same commit.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Single source of truth: identity types are reused from Phase 1; no parallel `FileId` or `SymbolId` definitions.\n- No broken windows: dead imports removed, every public name expresses intent; `ExcludedMemory.exclusion_reason` is non-optional.\n- Doc citation: the state-field table cites `## 5. Working Memory`; the checkpoint table placement cites `## Storage Design` and `## Phase 5: Working Memory` per `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every one of the eleven spec-named fields is in `WorkingMemoryState`; the `working_memory_checkpoints` table is created with versioned schema and deterministic hash.\n- [ ] `excluded_memories` carries a typed `exclusion_reason` — the \"and reasons\" spec requirement is enforced by the type system, not by convention.\n- [ ] Save/load round-trips a checkpoint byte-identically (regression test pinned).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3804,"cache_read_input_tokens":67149,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3804,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019RjXwCuf57BAKn88mWkE24","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"34b5d4e7-9771-4353-b726-5d17e43a83af","request_id":"req_011Cb7NAia285c59JysE8vFy","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 4+5 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T71.log:8009:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T41.log:1966:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2733:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:758:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_019JjqMQ778TToFmau2PwXFQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TKbr7KNkKbifeCtRHe4Xvy","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T62.md","content":"# T62 — Phase 8 — Workflow outcome recording integrated by default + composition tests\n\n**Phase:** 8\n**Type:** tests\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T56, T57, T58, T59, T60, T61\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-8-workflow-engine-v2)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task wires automatic workflow-outcome recording into every redesigned tool from T56–T60 and ships the composition test suite that proves the new tools compose correctly over the underlying substrates. Spec §Phase 8 Deliverables names \"workflow outcome recording integrated by default\" as a binding deliverable. Spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles requires \"every workflow tool should record inputs, resolved anchors, selected candidates, excluded high-scoring candidates where useful, response summary, downstream use events.\" Without this task, individual tools work in isolation but the event log + memory consolidation cannot observe whether a workflow succeeded, which breaks Phase 6 procedure extraction and Phase 9 success metrics.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 8 Deliverables: …workflow outcome recording integrated by default.\" (spec §Phase 8)\n\n> \"Every workflow tool should record: inputs; resolved anchors; selected candidates; excluded high-scoring candidates where useful; response summary; downstream use events.\" (spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles)\n\n> \"Required event types: …`WorkflowSucceeded`, `WorkflowFailed`…\" (spec §Event Log)\n\n> \"Definition of done: completed tasks produce useful episode memories; repeated successful traces produce procedural memories; consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered.\" (spec §Phase 6 — depends on outcome capture from this task)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T56 (`workflow_v2/` edit-planning tools).\n  - T57 (`workflow_v2/` discovery tools).\n  - T58–T60 (`memory_v2/` tools).\n  - T61 (final MCP tool reference).\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` — Phase 2 event writer, including `WorkflowSucceeded` and `WorkflowFailed` envelopes.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/record_workflow_outcome.rs` — current explicit outcome recorder, retained as a manual override path.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/` — every tool from T56/T57 to be wrapped with the outcome interceptor.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/memory_v2/` — every tool from T58/T59/T60 to be wrapped with the outcome interceptor.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/session_metrics.rs` — outcome events feed session metrics.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Hard limits, §Single source of truth, §No broken windows, §Schema parity, §Observability, §Tests as documentation; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Phase 8, §MCP Tool Contract Principles, and §Event Log in full. Quote the \"Every workflow tool should record…\" paragraph verbatim into a doc comment on `workflow_v2/outcome_capture.rs` so the contract is enforced at the call site.\n2. Implement `workflow_v2/outcome_capture.rs` as a reusable interceptor: a `WorkflowOutcomeRecorder` type that wraps any tool handler, captures inputs, resolved anchors, selected candidates, excluded candidates (the top-N high-scoring candidates that were rejected and why), response summary, and emits a `WorkflowSucceeded` or `WorkflowFailed` event on completion. The recorder must run on the hot path within the spec §Phase 2 budget (≤5ms P99 overhead).\n3. Wire `WorkflowOutcomeRecorder` into every redesigned tool from T56 and T57 by default. Wire it into every memory tool from T58/T59/T60 that participates in a user-facing workflow (excluding `get_event_trace`, which would create a feedback loop). Default-on means the tool registration in `mcp.rs` constructs the recorder around the handler; the legacy explicit `record_workflow_outcome` tool remains available as a manual override per the T02 compatibility policy.\n4. The recorder must also feed `session_metrics` so success rate, irrelevant-files-opened, and `workflow_success_after_first_plan` (from spec §Phase 9) are accumulated automatically.\n5. Define how \"downstream use events\" are tracked: when a returned context handle or memory ID is subsequently referenced in another tool call, the recorder back-links the later event to the originating workflow via the Phase 2 `EventEnvelope::references` field.\n6. In `workflow_v2/composition_tests.rs`, write composition tests that exercise the new tools end-to-end against a fixture workspace: (a) `prepare_change → plan_edit → apply_patch_simulated → record outcome` chain emits the correct sequence of events; (b) `get_context_capsule → expand_context` chain returns stable handle deltas without redundant payload; (c) `diagnose_failure → prepare_change` chain reuses anchors from the failure diagnosis; (d) `save_memory → verify_explain_memory` chain transitions an unverified memory to a verified state and emits the matching events; (e) `consolidate_session → propose_memory_evolution(action=\"apply\")` chain applies a proposal that the session produced; (f) every workflow chain emits exactly one `WorkflowSucceeded` or `WorkflowFailed` event per outer call; (g) excluded high-scoring candidates appear in the recorded outcome with rejection reasons.\n7. Assert that the hot-path budget is honored: composition tests measure the per-tool overhead added by `WorkflowOutcomeRecorder` and fail if it exceeds the spec §Phase 2 ≤5ms P99 budget.\n8. Assert that scope-leak and workspace-boundary protections from T58–T60 are preserved when the recorder is in the path.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/outcome_capture.rs` — `WorkflowOutcomeRecorder` interceptor capturing inputs, resolved anchors, selected/excluded candidates, response summary, and emitting `WorkflowSucceeded` / `WorkflowFailed` events per spec §MCP Tool Contract Principles; wired by default into every T56–T60 tool.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/composition_tests.rs` — end-to-end composition tests covering chained tool calls, handle stability, anchor reuse, verification transitions, consolidation→apply chains, outcome emission, excluded-candidate recording, and hot-path budget.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/outcome_capture.rs` — confirms the recorder file exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/workflow_v2/composition_tests.rs` — confirms the composition test file exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::workflow_v2::composition_tests` — every composition test passes (event ordering, handle stability, anchor reuse, verification transition, consolidation→apply, excluded-candidate recording, hot-path budget, scope-leak preservation).\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: the recorded outcome payload uses the same `WorkflowSucceeded` / `WorkflowFailed` envelopes defined in Phase 2; no parallel outcome struct is introduced.\n- No silent error handling — recorder failures emit `tracing::warn!` AND surface as `RiskNote`s in the bundle, never swallowed.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`WorkflowOutcomeRecorder::record_excluded`, not `WorkflowOutcomeRecorder::also_excl`).\n- For SQLite: outcome persistence goes through the Phase 2 event writer; no raw SQL in the RPC layer.\n- Observability: the recorder emits one `tracing::info!` span per recorded outcome with `tool`, `task_id`, `session_id`, `outcome`, `excluded_count`, `latency_ms` fields.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2`, `## MCP Tool Contract Principles`, `## Event Log`, and `## Phase 2 — Event Log Substrate` Definition of Done (hot-path budget) in module doc comments.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: the recorder is wired by default into every T56–T60 tool eligible for outcome capture; composition tests cover every chained scenario named in the steps; the hot-path budget assertion is live, not commented out.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed; the legacy explicit `record_workflow_outcome` tool continues to respond per the T02 compatibility policy.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3649,"cache_read_input_tokens":81273,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3649,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":54,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"729bf2f0-fa94-4854-a179-85471920cbc3","request_id":"req_011Cb7NBPjimQ7hZ8gS7hpnH","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T71.log:8010:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T41.log:1969:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2736:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:782:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_019ScAFhwj15KgrT4jB8Uvi8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TwdU3puoByR54shmvCc2Fc","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/R78.md","content":"# R78 — Frontend review — Phase 10 human review UI (UI-spec compliance)\n\n**Phase:** Review for Phase 10\n**Type:** review\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** T71, T72, T73, T74, T75, T76, T77\n**Spec anchor:** [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-10-human-review-and-extension-ux)\n**Standards reviewed:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); UI specification (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md`); definition-of-done checklist (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md`).\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis review certifies that the Phase 10 human review UI ships compliant with the shared UI specification, the Lattice coding standard, the spec's mandatory operator view set, and the i18n discipline that all user-facing strings live in a single catalog. It is the gate that R79 (contract gate) and R88 (definition-of-done gate) depend on: a frontend that bypasses `<dialog>`, swallows errors, or inlines English literals would force later phases into rework. Passing this review means the review surface is enterprise-grade on day one per the execution philosophy in `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Inputs to read\n\n- Every artifact produced by T71–T77:\n  - `extension/src/review/reviewPanel.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/i18n/en.json`\n  - `extension/src/review/i18n/index.ts` (i18n helper)\n  - `extension/src/review/memoryInbox.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/components/MemoryRow.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/components/StatusBadge.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/promotionQueue.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/contradictionQueue.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/components/ProposalDialog.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/staleView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/evidenceInspector.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/eventTraceView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/retrievalExplanationView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/consolidationQueueView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/indexingHealthView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/review/workspaceGraphHealthView.ts`\n  - `extension/src/test/review.test.ts`\n  - `extension/src/test/runTest.ts`\n  - `extension/package.json` (review view + command contribution diffs)\n- Spec sections this phase claims to satisfy: `## 10. Human Review Surface`, `## Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX`.\n- Shared standards: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (hard limits, error handling, single source of truth), `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md` (all sections — §3 accessibility, §6 lists & tables, §7 modals, §8 state management, §11 visual standards, §14 i18n, §16 telemetry), `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md`.\n\n## Review procedure\n\n1. Open every file produced by T71–T77 and confirm the eleven operator views from spec §10 are all reachable from `ReviewPanelProvider`: memory inbox, proposed promotions, proposed contradictions, stale memory list, memory evidence view, event trace view, retrieval explanation view, usefulness metrics, workspace graph health, indexing health, consolidation queue. (Usefulness metrics may live in the existing sidebar from `sidebar.ts` if R70 confirmed that surface; either way the link must be reachable.)\n2. Grep every `.ts` file under `extension/src/review/` for `window.confirm(`, `window.prompt(`, `window.alert(` — every hit is a blocker per `ui-specification.md` §7. Grep for `<dialog` to confirm the modals from T73 and T75 use native `<dialog>` elements.\n3. Grep every `.ts` file under `extension/src/review/` for inline English literals in JSX-equivalent strings: look for quoted strings inside webview HTML template literals that are not wrapped in `i18n.t(...)`. Any hit is a blocker per `ui-specification.md` §14.\n4. Open `extension/src/review/i18n/en.json` and confirm namespaces exist for: panel, statusBadge, memoryInbox, promotionQueue, contradictionQueue, proposalDialog, staleView, evidenceInspector, eventTraceView, retrievalExplanationView, consolidationQueueView, indexingHealthView, workspaceGraphHealthView. Spot-check that every column header, action label, empty state, and error state from the eleven views has a corresponding key.\n5. Open every mutation handler (accept/reject in `ProposalDialog`, re-verify in `staleView`/`evidenceInspector`, retry in `consolidationQueueView`, recompute in `workspaceGraphHealthView`) and confirm each is wrapped in `try/catch` with `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` plus an inline error state. Silent `.catch(() => {})` or bare `await` without surrounding handler is a blocker per `ui-specification.md` §8.5 and `coding.md` §Error handling.\n6. Confirm `StatusBadge.ts` covers all eight verification statuses from spec §8 (`verified`, `unverified`, `in_review`, `stale`, `contradicted`, `superseded`, `expired`, `invalidated`) and every additional badge family (proposal status, job status, decision badge in retrieval explanation). Confirm the file is the only place `verification-status → color/label` mapping lives.\n7. Open every table (memory inbox, promotion queue, contradiction queue, stale view, event trace, candidate table in retrieval explanation, consolidation queue, per-family counts in graph health) and confirm sortable columns use the `SortableHeader` + `useTableSort` equivalents exposed by T71 (per `ui-specification.md` §6.2). Confirm pagination is present on every list per `ui-specification.md` §6.1.\n8. Open every interactive surface and confirm it renders loading, empty, and error states explicitly (per `ui-specification.md` §8.2, §8.3, §8.5).\n9. Grep for `prompt(`, raw `<select>`, and `type=\"number\"` ID inputs — any hit for ID-bearing fields is a blocker. Confirm filter pickers use `vscode.window.showQuickPick`.\n10. Run `wc -l` on every file produced by T71–T77 and confirm no file exceeds 800 lines per `coding.md`.\n11. Grep all files for `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error` without justification — each hit is a blocker per `coding.md` §No broken windows.\n12. Run `cd extension && npm install && npm run compile && npm run lint && npm test` and confirm everything passes.\n13. Cross-check that the smoke tests from T77 cover every subview from T72–T76 (activation, view registration, mount, empty data, bridge error).\n\n## Required sections in the review output file\n\nThe reviewer writes to `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` with these mandatory headings (each must contain real findings, not \"n/a\"):\n\n- `## Spec alignment` — phase-by-phase coverage matrix for spec §10: one row per required operator view (eleven views), evidence column citing the file/function that delivers the view, status PASS / PASS-WITH-CAVEATS / BLOCK.\n- `## Coding-standard alignment` — line-by-line audit vs. `coding.md` hard limits: file lengths (`wc -l` table for every Phase 10 file), function-length grep, forbidden-token grep (`TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, unjustified suppressions), error-handling audit (every mutation `try/catch` confirmed), single-source-of-truth audit (status-badge mapping in one place, i18n catalog as single source).\n- `## UI-specification compliance` — walk every section of `ui-specification.md` that applies to the review UI in order. Each subsection states the rule, lists the artifacts checked, and the verdict:\n  - §3 Accessibility baseline — focus management, keyboard, ARIA, target size, focus rings, live regions on status badges.\n  - §6 Lists & tables — pagination present on every list; sortable headers via `SortableHeader` + `useTableSort` equivalents; column standards.\n  - §7 Modals & dialogs — every modal native `<dialog>`; no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`; typed-confirm on high-scope destructive actions in `ProposalDialog`.\n  - §8 State management — loading, empty, error states on every surface; every mutation has `try/catch` + `vscode.window.showErrorMessage`; error message style obeys Nielsen #9.\n  - §11 Visual standards — status badges use the single shared component; semantic color tokens, not hard-coded colors.\n  - §12 Data display — dates via `Intl.DateTimeFormat`; truncation on long content.\n  - §14 Internationalization — all user-facing strings via `i18n.t(...)`; no inline literals; ICU placeholders on interpolated strings; no string concatenation for user text.\n  - §16 Telemetry-friendly markup — `data-testid` on every interactive element; format `data-testid=\"review-<view>-<action>\"`.\n- `## i18n coverage` — table of every user-facing string the review UI emits, with the i18n key and the namespace; flag any inline literal that escaped catalog wrapping as a blocker.\n- `## Findings` — issues found, severity (`blocker`, `major`, `minor`), suggested follow-up tasks. Follow-up tasks must be opened in this build (do not defer outside).\n- `## Verdict` — `PASS`, `PASS-WITH-FOLLOWUP-TASK-Txx`, or `BLOCK` (with named blockers).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms the review markdown exists where downstream tasks can read it.\n- `grep -q '## Spec alignment' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms spec alignment is a dedicated section, not prose.\n- `grep -q '## UI-specification compliance' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms UI-spec compliance walked explicitly.\n- `grep -q '## i18n coverage' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms i18n coverage table present.\n- `grep -q '## Verdict' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R78-frontend.md` — confirms the review concludes with a binding verdict.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Review markdown file exists at the path verification commands check.\n- [ ] Every required section above is present with concrete content (not \"n/a\").\n- [ ] `## Coding-standard alignment` and `## Findings` headings are also present (not gated by tasks.json verification but required for completeness).\n- [ ] If verdict is BLOCK, the blocking issues are listed and a follow-up task is opened in this build (never deferred to a future session per `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` execution philosophy).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":101320,"cache_read_input_tokens":10845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":101320,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"92e01477-56ca-4f53-90e6-aa05679be5ea","request_id":"req_011Cb7NGicAyFMeCwEAwKwU2","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T71.log:8011:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T41.log:1971:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2738:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:788:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_013zgGbBpL5RCG9X6xGX3GFk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LLzhtrw7NNpnBWtrst5QqD","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T36.md","content":"# T36 — Phase 5 — MCP surface for working memory inspection + event capture for include/exclude reasons\n\n**Phase:** 5\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T34, T35\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 5: Working Memory](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-5-working-memory), [§5. Working Memory](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#5-working-memory), [§3. Event Log](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#3-event-log), [§9. MCP Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#9-mcp-surface)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers the MCP tool that lets a client inspect the current `WorkingMemoryState` and the event-capture hooks that automatically log included and excluded context per spec §Phase 5 deliverable (\"automatic event capture for included and excluded context\"). The MCP tool exposes the same compact-by-default shape the rest of the MCP surface uses; the event hooks emit `MemoryRetrieved` and `MemoryExpanded` events (Phase 2 event kinds) every time the working memory's `selected_memories` or `excluded_memories` change. Together these make working memory observable — meeting the Phase 5 Definition of Done (\"workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\").\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 5: Working Memory — Deliverables: ... MCP surface for inspecting current working memory; automatic event capture for included and excluded context ...\" (spec §Phase 5: Working Memory)\n\n> \"Required event types: ... ContextBundleReturned; MemoryRetrieved; MemoryExpanded ...\" (spec §3. Event Log)\n\n> \"Definition of done: workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded; context management is no longer just prompt accumulation.\" (spec §Phase 5: Working Memory)\n\n> \"Every tool response should support: compact rendering; full structured JSON; context handles; stable expansion targets; budget controls.\" (spec §9. MCP Surface)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T34 `WorkingMemoryState` and checkpoint API.\n  - T35 `OpContext`, eight operations, and `record_state_mutation` internal hook.\n  - Phase 2 event-log API — exposes `MemoryRetrieved`, `MemoryExpanded`, `ContextBundleReturned` writers.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs` — JSON-RPC server registration.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — `McpHandler::handle_tools_call()` dispatch and tool-schema declarations.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/context_cache.rs` — context-handle convention for round-trippable handles.\n  - Phase 2 event writer (assumed to live at `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/`).\n- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, single source of truth — reuse the existing MCP dispatch convention); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` (compact-by-default; build/deploy procedure for the daemon).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Re-read spec §Phase 5 deliverables, §3. Event Log event-kind list, and §9. MCP Surface response contract to fix the tool shape and event-emission contract.\n2. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool.rs`: register a new MCP tool `inspect_working_memory` with schema `{ task_id: string, mode: \"compact\" | \"diagnostic\", include_excluded: boolean }`. The handler loads the active `WorkingMemoryState` for the task (from the session-scoped in-memory store maintained by T35's `OpContext`, falling back to the latest checkpoint if the session has rolled), then returns either a compact summary (via T35 `summarize`) or the full state including `excluded_memories` when `include_excluded=true`. Response carries an `expansion_handle` per spec §9 so the caller can expand the full state without re-fetching.\n3. Wire the tool into `mcp.rs` dispatch alongside the existing 43 tools. Tool registration includes the schema in the MCP `tools/list` response; failure to expose a parameter is a contract violation caught by the cross-layer MCP schema test gate.\n4. Implement `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/event_hooks.rs`: a small module exposing `emit_memory_retrieved(state_before, state_after, op_summary, writer)` and `emit_memory_expanded(state_before, state_after, op_summary, writer)`. Each function computes the diff (which identities were added to `selected_memories`, which were added to `excluded_memories` with reasons), packages the diff into an event payload per Phase 2's event-payload contract (workspace, branch, session, task, actor, timestamp, payload hash, compact summary, optional full payload location), and writes through the Phase 2 event writer.\n5. Attach the hooks to T35's `record_state_mutation` so every state-mutating operation emits the appropriate event automatically: `retrieve` and `compress` emit `MemoryRetrieved` (since they decide what is included/excluded); `expand` emits `MemoryExpanded`; `pin`, `evict`, `filter`, `checkpoint` emit `MemoryRetrieved` when they change inclusion/exclusion. `summarize` emits no event.\n6. Honor the spec invariant \"the full event stream should not be injected into prompts\" — the event hooks never put event data back into `WorkingMemoryState`. Events flow one way: state mutation → event log.\n7. Hot-path discipline: event writes share the existing Phase 2 write budget (`≤5ms P99`). If the writer returns a backpressure error, the operation does not fail — the event is dropped with a `warn!` log naming the task and operation, per `coding.md` \"no silent .catch\" but tolerant of event-log overload.\n8. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool_tests.rs` covering: tool registration appears in `tools/list`; compact mode returns a summary; diagnostic mode returns the full state and embedded `RetrievalBundle`-shaped `selected_memories`; `include_excluded=true` returns excluded entries with reasons; expansion handle round-trips back to the same state; a synthetic event writer captures one `MemoryRetrieved` event per `retrieve` call and one `MemoryExpanded` event per `expand` call; event payload carries the included and excluded identity diffs and reasons.\n9. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release` (release build verifies the whole daemon still compiles with the new tool registered), then `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::working_memory_tool_tests`. Deploy the new daemon binary per the project rule (`pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`) only when explicitly told to ship; the test commands themselves do not require a redeploy.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool.rs` — MCP tool registration, schema, handler, dispatch wiring.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/working_memory/event_hooks.rs` — `emit_memory_retrieved`, `emit_memory_expanded`, state-diff computation, event payload assembly.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool_tests.rs` — in-tree tests for tool registration, compact/diagnostic modes, exclude visibility, expansion handle, event capture.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/working_memory_tool.rs`\n- `cd daemon && cargo build --release`\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-daemon --lib rpc::working_memory_tool_tests`\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. The MCP handler decomposes mode handling into helpers; event-diff computation is its own function.\n- Schema parity: the MCP tool JSON schema mirrors the Rust handler's parameter struct exactly; the response shape mirrors `WorkingMemoryState` (compact view exposes a documented subset). Schema declaration and Rust struct are updated in the same commit.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Single source of truth: state loading uses T34's `load_checkpoint` / T35's `OpContext` session store; no parallel state lookup. Event writing uses the Phase 2 event writer; no parallel event payload assembler.\n- No broken windows: dead imports removed; the backpressure-drop path logs at `warn!` with task and operation context, never silent.\n- Doc citation: tool comments cite `## 9. MCP Surface`; event-hook comments cite `## 3. Event Log` and `## Phase 5: Working Memory` per `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: tool registration, compact/diagnostic modes, exclude visibility, expansion handle, automatic event capture all delivered in this task.\n- [ ] Every Phase-5 state mutation emits an event without manual call sites — the `record_state_mutation` hook is the single attachment point.\n- [ ] Backpressure on the event log degrades to a logged drop, not a failed operation.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3458,"cache_read_input_tokens":74783,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3458,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":51,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_019RjXwCuf57BAKn88mWkE24","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"4430d74f-dcef-44ba-9df8-04d1b33b60f6","request_id":"req_011Cb7NHj1kpRYTTxArY2nhh","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 4+5 task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T71.log:8012:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T41.log:1972:docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/T04.log:2739:./docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:797:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_019ScAFhwj15KgrT4jB8Uvi8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DeEj2PHYWT2tYJ3BkufH5S","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/R79.md","content":"# R79 — Contract gate — Extension ↔ Daemon review-UI MCP plumbing\n\n**Phase:** Contract gate after Phase 10\n**Type:** review\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R64, R78\n**Spec anchor:** [§9. MCP Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#9-mcp-surface), [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§MCP Tool Contract Principles](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#mcp-tool-contract-principles)\n**Standards reviewed:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis review is the contract gate between the Phase 10 review UI and the daemon MCP surface that powers it. Every method on `ReviewRpcBridge` calls a tool registered on the daemon side; this gate verifies that every method has a matching tool, that the JSON shapes round-trip end to end, that the schema is documented and regression-tested, and that no surface added by Phases 6–9 was forgotten on the extension side. The gate exists because UI compile + lint cannot detect contract drift — only an integration test that sends a real request to a real daemon and asserts the response shape can. Spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties demands \"every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested\"; this review enforces that for the review UI surface.\n\n## Inputs to read\n\n- Every artifact produced by T71–T77 (extension review surface).\n- Every artifact produced by Phase 6–9 MCP work the review UI depends on, including the R64 contract gate output for the MCP tool schema.\n- Existing files this review cross-checks:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` — registered MCP tools.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs` — JSON-RPC server.\n  - `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts` — typed extension-side bridge.\n  - `extension/src/daemon.ts` — underlying JSON-RPC client.\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` (from T02) — backward-compatibility policy this gate enforces.\n- Spec sections: `## 9. MCP Surface`, `## 10. Human Review Surface`, `## MCP Tool Contract Principles`.\n- Shared standards: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (schema parity, single source of truth); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md` (backward-compatibility discipline).\n\n## Review procedure\n\n1. Enumerate every method exposed by `ReviewRpcBridge` and confirm each maps to a registered MCP tool in `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs`. Any orphan method on either side is a blocker.\n2. For every mapped pair, inspect the typed request and response shapes on the TypeScript side (`rpcBridge.ts`) and the corresponding Rust schema in `mcp.rs` (and its serde structs). Field-by-field comparison: type, required/optional, name. Drift is a blocker per `coding.md` §Schema parity.\n3. Cross-check `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-mcp-compatibility-policy.md` (T02) — every review-UI tool is either `stable`, `additive`, or `redesigned-with-shim`; nothing is undocumented.\n4. Create `extension/src/test/contract.test.ts` (this is the deliverable contract test). The test launches a real daemon process via the existing pattern in `extension/src/test/`, sends one round-trip request per review-UI bridge method, and asserts the response parses into the typed shape. Include happy-path requests and an error-path request per tool. Tests use the real daemon binary (`daemon/target/release/lattice`), not a mock.\n5. Run the contract suite with `cd extension && npm install && npm run compile && npm test`. Confirm every round-trip passes. If the daemon binary is not present in `extension/bin/`, the test setup builds and copies per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy: `cd daemon && cargo build --release && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/`.\n6. Confirm the contract test exercises every review-UI tool from `rpcBridge.ts`: memory listing, evidence inspection, promotion queue read + apply + reject, contradiction queue read + apply + reject, stale listing, verify_memory, event trace read, retrieval explanation read, consolidation queue read + retry, indexing health read, graph health read + recompute.\n7. Grep `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/mcp.rs` for the corresponding tool registrations and confirm each accepts the documented request shape and returns the documented response shape.\n8. Cross-check that backward-compat shims from R64 (if any) are still in place: every legacy tool name continues to route to the new implementation.\n\n## Required sections in the review output file\n\nThe reviewer writes to `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R79-contract-extension-daemon.md` with these mandatory headings (each must contain real findings, not \"n/a\"):\n\n- `## Contract surfaces` — table of every `ReviewRpcBridge` method, the corresponding daemon tool name, the request shape, the response shape, and the spec citation. One row per method.\n- `## Round-trip evidence` — for every method, the contract-test name in `extension/src/test/contract.test.ts` that exercises it; the test output snippet showing the request, the response, and the assertion that the typed shape parsed; the daemon log line showing the request was received.\n- `## Schema surfaces` — TypeScript ↔ Rust field comparison table per tool. Columns: TS field, TS type, TS required, Rust field, Rust type, Rust required, parity verdict. Any drift is flagged.\n- `## Findings` — issues found, severity (`blocker`, `major`, `minor`), suggested follow-up tasks. Follow-up tasks must be opened in this build (do not defer outside).\n- `## Verdict` — `PASS`, `PASS-WITH-FOLLOWUP-TASK-Txx`, or `BLOCK` (with named blockers).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R79-contract-extension-daemon.md` — confirms the review markdown exists where downstream tasks can read it.\n- `test -f extension/src/test/contract.test.ts` — confirms the new contract test file exists.\n- `grep -q '## Contract surfaces' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R79-contract-extension-daemon.md` — confirms contract surfaces enumerated under a dedicated heading.\n- `grep -q '## Round-trip evidence' docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/R79-contract-extension-daemon.md` — confirms round-trip evidence is present.\n- `cd extension && npm run compile` — TypeScript compile (including the new contract test) succeeds.\n- `cd extension && npm test` — the contract suite plus all prior tests pass against a real daemon.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Review markdown file exists at the path verification commands check.\n- [ ] `extension/src/test/contract.test.ts` exists, compiles, and passes against the real daemon binary.\n- [ ] Every required section above is present with concrete content (not \"n/a\").\n- [ ] `## Schema surfaces` and `## Findings` are also present (not gated by tasks.json verification but required for completeness).\n- [ ] If verdict is BLOCK, the blocking issues are listed and a follow-up task is opened in this build (never deferred to a future session per `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` execution philosophy).\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":101320,"cache_read_input_tokens":10845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":101320,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"ce6e8520-9b5f-42b8-a09b-8be0a2ce22df","request_id":"req_011Cb7NGicAyFMeCwEAwKwU2","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
